🔺 **F-2639-1 — THE PRICED, TWICE-DEFERRED F-2569-3 CURE IS CLAIMED: `lane-usable`’S `DIRTY` BANNER NOW SAYS WHAT A REFILL WOULD ACTUALLY *LOSE* — AND THE THREE WORDS OF SCOPE IN ITS OWN NAMING TURNED OUT TO BE THE LOAD-BEARING HALF (measured and cured s2639, dry board, 104th consecutive; CODEX-WALL standing, owner POLICY, read AT SOURCE).** `lane-usable` printed `=> DIRTY: ... find its owner before touching this lane` — a CONTENTION verdict, correct and complete about its own question — while a fire reading it on a dry board is asking a DIFFERENT one: *what would a refill destroy?* The fuse is CODE, not conjecture: `lane-runner-v3.sh` refreshes a lane with `git reset --hard main`. F-2569-1 named the cure, F-2610-1 re-affirmed it unclaimed, and both deferred it for a stated COST — behaviour-neutrality proof plus its own guard plus the full battery — which is a price, never a constraint (the §2.0b lesson: a predecessor’s reason for declining is a CLAIM). 📊 **LIVE READING, REPRODUCING F-2610-1’S INDEPENDENTLY-BANKED HAND MEASUREMENT TO THE DIGIT — which is the strongest evidence the instrument is right, because that figure was taken by a different fire with a different method: `lane-d` `26` tracked-dirt path(s), `26` hashed from main, `SAFE 26 · LOCAL-REF-ONLY 0 · UNREFERENCED 0 · AT RISK 0`.** ⚖️ **IT DECLARES AND DOES NOT CLEAR (F-2366-1’s choice for BUSY, taken here for the same reason): the verdict word and exit code are untouched, `DIRTY` still exits 2. A false NOT-dirty is the Reset Massacre direction (Mistake #2), and F-2610-1’s whole lesson is that conflating the two questions IS the defect.** ✅ **BEHAVIOUR-NEUTRAL BEFORE LANDING (F-1274-2): `5/5` modes stripped-identical, rc unchanged, verdicts unmoved, control asserting its own validity first (F-2215-1 — the pre-cure arm really produced 27,935 B).** 💰 **PRICED BY INTERLEAVED A/B, NOT BY AN ADJACENT PAIR (F-2620-2): median `16.36 s → 18.98 s` on `--all`, **+16%**, load average `7.11` on 16 CPUs (named per F-24