🔺 **F-2638-1 — F-2636-1’S ONE-FIRE-OLD DUTY ASKS *“WHICH TREES LEFT?”* AND BANKS A **COUNT**, WHICH CANNOT ANSWER IT — AND NEITHER CAN THE DISK, BECAUSE DE-REGISTRATION REMOVES THE ONE MARKER THAT WOULD (measured s2638, on the first fall after the duty landed).** 📊 **THE FALL IS REAL AND WAS MEASURED WITH THE DUTY’S OWN INSTRUMENT, control asserting its own validity first (F-2215-1 — the census resolved `37641` tracked · `129114` remote-reachable · `190452` all-ref-reachable and answered `109 of 109`, so a divergence is about the registry and not about a failed read): s2637 banked `corpus 110 registered`; I measured **`corpus 109`**. The census’s `trees.length` is the RAW, unfiltered `git worktree list --porcelain` count (read at `scripts/modified-tracked-evidence-census.mjs:203–206`), so there is no filter to explain it away, and the `.git/worktrees` admin dirs fell with it — **`108` admin dirs against `108` linked entries, `prunable 0`** — which is F-2636-1’s own discriminator for a REAL removal rather than a display filter.** 🕳️ **THEN THE DUTY’S QUESTION PROVED UNANSWERABLE, AND NOT FOR WANT OF TRYING — I took it three ways and all three fail BY CONSTRUCTION. (1) A COUNT names nothing: `110 → 109` is one number, and the departed path is not recoverable from it. (2) A `.git`-keyed disk sweep returns **ZERO** candidates repo-wide, because a de-registered tree HAS no `.git`. (3) A path-shape sweep returns **49** unregistered scratchpad dirs and cannot rank them.** 🔑 **THE MEASUREMENT THAT SETTLES IT, and it is four `existsSync` calls: all five F-2636-1-named de-registered trees (`wt-maps` · `wt-boss` · `wt-boss-control` · `wt-maps-control` · `wt-sprites-control`) have **NO `.git`** — and neither do the plain scratch dirs beside them (`ztmp` · `tools` · `vite-cache-5360` · `debris`). **On disk a de-registered worktree and an ordinary directory are THE SAME SHAPE.** De-registration erases the only evidence that the thing was ever a worktree, so the departure is unnameable the moment it happens, forever, by anyone.** ⚖️ **SEVERITY STATED HONESTLY AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED: realised cost ZERO, NOTHING WAS LOST, and this is NOT a false green.** The desk figure is **BYTE-IDENTICAL** to s2637 (`482 file(s) / 447.9 MB / 25 tree(s)`, `0` factory-side), so the departed tree contributed **nothing** to the AT RISK bucket; and all five HOLDING trees are still whole — re-walked from disk with `lstat` and symlinks SKIPPED (F-2607-1’s method note): **`1747` evidence files, the pre-prune count to the digit** (`wt-maps` `1672` / `926.0` MB · `wt-boss-control` `36` · `wt-sprites-control` `23` · `wt-maps-control` `15` · `wt-boss` `1`), totalling `1001.2` MB and reproducing F-2617-1’s original figure exactly. ✅ **AND F-2637-1’S UNIT CURE HELD ON ITS FIRST EXERCISE, which is the cleanest corroboration available: I divided by `1e6` and got `926.0` matching the bank, while the MiB column of the same walk reads `883.1` — precisely the phantom 4.9% drop s2637 reported on itself.** 🎯 **WHAT EARNS IT A CLAUSE IS THE DIRECTION AND THE PERMANENCE: F-2636-1 exists because a de-registered tree goes invisible to every instrument at once (they all take their subject set from the registry), and its whole remedy is *“a path is the only handle left”*. But a fire can only write down a path it can NAME — so on the very first fall the duty could detect the event and not describe it, and a successor reading `110 → 109` learns that something left and can never learn what.** 🛠️ **CURED BY MECHANISM, because the answer must be PERSISTED BEFORE THE FALL by the fire that can still see the tree — a count persisted before the fall does not help, a SET does: `node scripts/worktree-registry-ledger.mjs` (`6f9806c92`) banks the sorted registry SET to the tracked `logs/worktree-registry.json` and prints **ADDED / REMOVED BY NAME**, judging every departure for surviving evidence (`STILL HOLDING EVIDENCE` with a file count and DECIMAL-MB bytes · `GONE FROM DISK TOO` · `UNVERIFIABLE`). Because the baseline is TRACKED, a successor can also `git diff` it and be HANDED the departed path rather than deducing it — which is the RETENTION LAW working for us rather than against us.** 🚫 **ADVISORY, exit 0 in every state by default, and the restraint is INHERITED rather than re-argued: creating and removing worktrees is LAWFUL, routine attended work, so a red would fire during ordinary correct operation and be excused into uselessness inside a week (F-1460-1, the `cross-engine` fate) — the same restraint F-2485-1 and F-2636-1 both took for this very hazard. `--strict` gives `1` = answered-and-CHANGED, `2` = could-not-answer, the convention `drain-block-check`, `dry-board-probe` and `master-shipped-classifier` already carry.** 🔒 **Guarded by `scripts/worktree-registry-ledger-guard.test.mjs` — 14 arms, rooted in `test:ledger-guards`; `gate-caller-audit` PASS.** 🔬 **TEETH, 10 variants manufactured on scratch copies with the real subject asserted sha256-IDENTICAL after every one: control GREEN, **ALL 14 ARMS red under at least one variant, ZERO decoration**, and every over-general cure is caught by exactly the reverse control built for it — `absent` baseline REFUSING reds **arm 9 ALONE**, `--strict` always exiting 1 reds **arm 7 ALONE**, an `unreadable` baseline read as no-change reds **arm 10 ALONE**, the MEBIBYTE divisor reds **arm 4 ALONE**, a cwd anchor instead of `--root` reds **arm 13 ALONE**, and a GONE tree reported as holding reds **arm 3 ALONE**.** ⚠️ **TWO METHOD NOTES PAID FOR IN THIS FIRE, AND THE FIRST IS WORTH MORE THAN THE CURE. (1) MY GUARD’S FIRST DRAFT MEASURED THE REAL REPO AND NOT ITS FIXTURES — `repoRoot()` anchors to the SCRIPT’S OWN DIRECTORY, never to cwd, so a fixture that merely sets `cwd` is aimed at the wrong subject. **12 of 14 arms failed LOUD, which is how I found it — but TWO PASSED VACUOUSLY, and one of those two ran `--update` AGAINST THE REAL BASELINE FILE.** A vacuous pass is bad; a vacuous pass that MUTATES its ow