g on the very fire that was citing it. **When you publish a range, say how many samples it rests on and whether they were consecutive — and prefer a FLOOR you can defend to a BAND you cannot.**

🔎 **F-2620-1 — TWO LAW CLAUSES GIVE OPPOSITE ADVICE ABOUT `law-pointer-guard --update`, AND THE DISCRIMINATOR THAT RECONCILES THEM IS ONE READ YOU ALREADY HAVE (measured s2620, the first fire to move the gazette pointer after F-ATT-2 landed).** F-ATT-2 (attended 2026-09-18) is unambiguous and correct: *"a pointer move and its baseline re-base land in ONE commit"* — s2619 re-grepped the gazette coordinate, did NOT run `--update`, and `law-pointer-guard` answered `NEW POINTER` so the mandated battery was RED for every reader for about an hour. F-2595-1 is equally correct and points the other way: `--update` does NOT re-pin one pointer, it **REBUILDS THE ENTIRE BASELINE FROM CURRENT STATE** (`law-pointer-guard.mjs:381–387`, every `resolved` pointer rewritten wholesale), so a fire that runs it blind **BLESSES EVERY DRIFTED POINTER AT ONCE** — silently converting a real `POINTER DRIFT` alarm into a green, which is the act that file’s own usage line forbids. ⚖️ **Read literally and together they are jointly unsatisfiable in the F-2568-1 sense: one says ALWAYS `--update` in your landing commit, the other says `--update` is the dangerous act.** ➡️ **THE RECONCILIATION IS MECHANICAL, COSTS NOTHING, AND IS MEASURED RATHER THAN ARGUED: run the guard BARE FIRST and COUNT what it reports, then `--update` only when the reported set is exactly the move you just made and verified by eye.** 📊 **MEASURED s2620, with the eye-check asserted before anything was blessed (I read line 1986 back and it printed the `e7bb88cf` `upgrade-clock` citation itself): bare guard → `FAIL — 1 law-surface problem(s)`, the single `NEW POINTER` being my own `1984 -> 1986`, with `pointers 86 (checked 84, illustrative 2, **known-rotten 0**)` and `instruments 86 (resolved 86, **dead 0**)`. After `--update`: `PASS`, and — this is the confirming read — **`git diff --stat scripts/law-pointer-baseline.json` is `1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)`, i.e. ONE pointer.** A fire that had blessed N drifted pointers would see `2N` changed lines and know it.** 🔑 **SO THE SAFETY CHECK IS A PAIR, AND EITHER HALF ALONE IS INSUFFICIENT: the BEFORE count tells you how many pointers `--update` is about to rewrite, and the AFTER diff tells you how many it actually did. The before-count can be read as reassurance only because `known-rotten 0` and `dead 0` establish that the guard had no suppressed complaints hiding under the one it printed.** ⚖️ **SEVERITY STATED HONESTLY AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED: this is NOT a defect report and nothing is broken — both clauses are correct, F-ATT-2’s cure is right, and the realised cost here was ZERO. It is a NEGATIVE RESULT banked with its mechanism (F-2214-1) because the next fire to move a pointer inherits a genuine tension and, with no procedure, resolves it by picking whichever clause it read last.** 🎯 **What earns it a row is the DIRECTION of each wrong choice: skip `--update` and you leave the battery RED for your successor (F-ATT-2’s measured hour); run it blind and you turn someone else’s real drift green, which is the failure no battery can ever report afterwards because the evidence is the thing you overwrote.** 🚫 **NO GUARD, and the restraint is INHERITED rather than re-argued: "did a fire read the guard’s count before running `--update`?" is unobservable to every battery — the same reason F-2567-1 declined one for a hand-typed clock, F-1669-1 for a filename read with the eyes, and F-2599-1 for a `| tail`. The cure is the sentence you are reading.** 💡 **REUSABLE, and it is F-2568-1’s shape on a new subject: that finding showed a DUTY and a GATE can be individually correct and jointly unsatisfiable, and that nothing in this factory examines PAIRS. This is the same defect between two LAW CLAUSES rather than a duty and a gate — and the resolution was not to overturn either one but to find the cheap observation that tells you which applies. **When two clauses you trust disagree, look for the measurement that separates the case each was written for, before you decide one of them is wrong.**

