⏳ **F-2612-1 — §1.3's BINDING RULE FOR JUDGING A LOCK'S AGE — *"judge by its OWNING COMMIT"* — HAS A THIRD FAILURE MODE ITS TEXT DOES NOT NAME: A **FOREIGN WRITER** EDITING LINE 1 IN PLACE RE-DATES THAT COMMIT, AND UNLIKE THE TWO IT DOES NAME, THIS ONE MAKES A LOCK LOOK **FRESHER** (fire s2612, dry board, 80th consecutive; CODEX-WALL standing, owner POLICY, read AT SOURCE).** §1.3 establishes that stamp TEXT is untrustworthy — hand-computed drift (F-1039-2, +14 to +56 min) and the local-vs-UTC mislabel (~7 h, both labelled `Z`) — and rules that the cure is to ask git instead: *"judge a lock's age by its OWNING COMMIT, not by the stamp text — `git log -1 --format=%cI` on the commit that **wrote line-1**."* **That phrase resolves to "the commit that last TOUCHED line 1", which is not the same thing as the commit that took the lock.** 📊 **MEASURED s2612 over the last `120` STATUS.md commits (`118` line-1 transitions), the control asserting its own validity first (F-2215-1 — all 120 resolved to a readable line 1 and 8 distinct owners were seen, so a zero would be an ANSWER and not a failed read): **`2` FOREIGN in-place rewrites (1.7%)** — a commit that changed line 1 while its OWNER token stayed the same and the writer was a different hand. `1362b9e3d` (2026-09-17, **the attended session rewriting s2611's line**) is a pure STAMP REFRESH, `22:27Z` → `16:40Z`, i.e. local-labelled-`Z` → true UTC, with no reason stated anywhere; and `347e73594` (2026-09-16, **s2586 rewriting the attended line**) is the F-2586-2 desk carry-back — **LAWFUL and in fact LAW-MANDATED**, since `desk-carryforward-guard` reddened until it happened.** ⚖️ **SEVERITY STATED HONESTLY AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED: realised cost ZERO, nothing is broken, NOT a false green, and **BOTH rewrites landed on `lock CLEARED` lines** — measured, not assumed — so the dangerous parameterisation has NEVER occurred and no lock has ever been misjudged by it. One of the two is a duty this factory requires.** 🎯 **What earns it a row is the DIRECTION at the one moment it could fire, and that it is the OPPOSITE of the two modes §1.3 names: arithmetic drift and a timezone mislabel both move a stamp by a KNOWN sign and are caught by asking git. A foreign edit moves the thing you asked git FOR. On an `ACTIVE` line it would rejuvenate a dead fire's lock — the stamp would read fresh AND the binding rule would read fresh — so §1.1's cure and §1.3's cure would AGREE on a corpse, and the exit §1.1 prescribes there is silent and self-perpetuating (~38 min, priced by F-2204-1).** ➡️ **THEREFORE: F-2204-1's `FIRE END` tail is not belt-and-braces here, it is the ONLY surviving discriminator — and when you judge a lock by its owning commit, read WHO WROTE that commit as well as WHEN. A line-1 commit signed by someone other than the lock's owner has re-dated the lock without touching the fire.** ⓘ **A second, inert observation banked rather than inflated: the refreshed stamp `16:40Z` is ~6 minutes AHEAD of its own commit (`16:34:42` UTC) — the future-dated stamp §1.3 prices as stalling the factory for *"the skew plus 45 minutes"*. On a CLEARED line it costs nothing. It is recorded only so nobody reads `16:40Z` as a correction of `22:27Z`; it is a fresh `date -u` reading, not an arithmetic fix.** 🚫 **NO GUARD, and the restraint is MEASURED rather than lazy: *"did a foreign writer edit line 1?"* selects a class that is **half law-mandated** — F-2586-2's carry-back is exactly this shape and `test:ledger-guards` REQUIRES it — so a red would fire during ordinary correct operation and be excused into uselessness inside a week (F-1460-1, the `cross-engine` fate). It is the same restraint F-1669-1 took for a filename read with the eyes and F-2567-1 for a hand-typed clock. The cure is the sentence you are reading, at the site.** ⚠️ **THE METHOD NOTE IS WORTH MORE THAN THE FINDING, AND IT NEARLY SHIPPED WRONG: my first classifier took the owner token as the first `sNNNN` **anywhere** in line 1 — and line 1 is a 12,588-character paragraph that CITES session ids in its own prose. That is F-2102-1's narration confound (where an unanchored `FIRE END` count inflated a day ≈2.7×) on a new subject: **a line NARRATING session ids was scored as being OWNED by them.** Re-keyed on the PREFIX alone (everything before the first em-dash) the classifier **MISLABELLED `8aa8a5b93`** — the attended session rewriting its OWN line, whose owner it read as `s2574` from a citation — **and MISSED `347e73594` entirely**. 🔑 **AND IT RETURNED THE SAME TOTAL BOTH TIMES: `2`. Two errors in opposite directions cancelled exactly, so the headline was right while the subject set was half wrong — and the half it invented (a stamp refresh by the same author) and the half it lost (the law-mandated desk carry-back) would have led to OPPOSITE conclusions about whether this class is lawful.** 💡 **REUSABLE, and it is a NEW AXIS for this streak: twenty-odd findings have asked how a measurement can be WRONG — a swallow, a guard, a cwd, a filename, a timezone, a registry, a clock, a load, an expiry. **THIS ONE WAS WRONG AND ITS TOTAL WAS RIGHT.** A count is a lossy projection of a subject set, so agreement between two methods' COUNTS is not evidence their SETS agree. When you fix a classifier, diff the members it returns, never the tally — and if the tally did not move, that is the moment to look hardest.**

⏳ **F-2611-1 — A DESK ITEM CAN LEAVE THE OWNER'S DESK *LAWFULLY* AND STILL RED THE CARRY-FORWARD GUARD, AND THE NEAREST PRECEDENT PRESCRIBES THE *OPPOSITE* CURE (fire s2611, dry board, 79th consecutive).** The attended session ruled **A21 (a)** on 2026-09-17 (owner, verbatim *"Lets do them all."*), authored `tasks/picnic-claim-standdown.md` (3326 B, `fcb198fb4`) and removed **`F-PLAY-E6-1`** from the desk — a RULING, which is one of the desk law's two lawful exits. But line-1 kept declaring **7 awaiting a word** while carrying **6**, and carried no `DESK-DROPPED:` acknowledgement. 📊 **MEASURED on scratch roots, the CONTROL asserting its own validity FIRST (F-2215-1): `desk-carryforward-guard` against s2610's line (declares 7, carries 7) → **rc=0 PASS**, *"dropped: 0"*; against the attended line (declares 7, carries 6) → **rc=1 FAIL**, naming `F-PLAY-E6-1` and printing the exact cure. So the red is attributable to that one drop and to nothing else. `desk-declaration-guard` reads **rc=0 on BOTH** — it asserts every desk id has a declaring BACKLOG row, and has no opinion on the header COUNT, so the count alone was never the gated thing.** ⚖️ **SEVERITY STATED HONESTLY AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED: realised cost ZERO, nothing is lost, and this is NOT a false green — the guard is LOUD and CORRECT, `STATUS.md` is tracked, and no ruling was missed. Nor is it an accusation: an attended session composing a handoff after ruling six uses at once is doing ordinary correct work, and the acknowledgement is a convention no gate could have shown it mid-compose (the desk gates SKIP while line 1 reads `ACTIVE`, F-2227-2, so this can only ever surface AFTER a handoff commit).** 🎯 **WHAT EARNS IT A ROW IS THAT THE OBVIOUS PRECEDENT IS THE WRONG ONE, AND IT IS THREE ROWS DOWN THIS SAME LEDGER. F-2586-2 is the same shape — a concurrent attended handoff dropping desk items — and its cure was to CARRY THEM BACK BYTE-VERBATIM, correctly, *because there was no ruling to honour* (it says so in its own words: *"nothing in the attended line-1 mentions the three ids, so there was no ruling"*). Here there IS one. A fire pattern-matching on F-2586-2 would carry `F-PLAY-E6-1` back and put an ALREADY-DECIDED question back in front of the owner — the opposite error, and the one the desk law's *"only lawful exits are a ruling or a closure"* exists to prevent.** 🔑 **AND THE TWO CASES ARE BYTE-IDENTICAL TO THE PARSER, WHICH IS WHY NO MECHANISM CAN SEPARATE THEM: `analyse()` excuses a drop only via `acknowledged(line1, id)` (a `DESK-DROPPED` mark within 400 chars) or `subjectLedClosure(backlog, id)`. A ruling that is real but written NOWHERE either instrument reads scores exactly as a silent drop. **"Left with no reason given" and "left with a reason nobody wrote down" are the same bytes and call for opposite acts** — so the discriminator is not in the text, it is in the WORLD: read `tasks/BACKLOG.md` line 1 for the ruling and check whether the master it names exists on disk. Both were true here.** ➡️ **THEREFORE, CURED THE WAY THE GUARD ITSELF PRESCRIBES rather than by carry-back: s2611's line-1 carries the 6 items BYTE-VERBATIM, declares **6**, and states `DESK-DROPPED: F-PLAY-E6-1 — ruled 2026-09-17 (a)` with the master named. Verified on a scratch root before committing, not after.** ⚠️ **ONE RESIDUE REPORTED AND DELIBERATELY NOT EDITED: `tasks/BACKLOG.md:49` still reads *"`F-PLAY-E6-1` … awaiting the owner's word"*, so the ledger states the item OPEN at one line and RULED at another. The §4 rule is that a superseded line retires in the SAME commit as the event, and the event here is the attended ruling, not my fire — that thread is live attended work (phase B lands the stand-down) and a fire editing another writer's in-flight row is the "one writer per surface" violation. It will retire when the stand-down lands; if it has not, whoever drains that slice owes it.** 🚫 **NO GUARD, and the restraint is INHERITED rather than re-argued: the guard that catches this ALREADY EXISTS, is already rooted, and already reddened — what failed was not DETECTION but the acknowledgement, and *"is this drop backed by a real ruling?"* is a judgement over the world that no parser can take (the file's own header reaches this conclusion twice, and F-2586-2 took the same restraint). A red on "line 1 changed hands" would fire on every lawful attended handoff and be excused into uselessness inside a week (F-1460-1, the `cross-engine` fate).** 💡 **REUSABLE, and it is a NEW AXIS: this streak's findings ask whether a MEASUREMENT is wrong — a swallow, a cwd, a filename, a clock, a registry, a truncation. Here the measurement is RIGHT, the guard is RIGHT, and **the guard's own printed REMEDY names one cure of two**. A failure message is written by someone who knew only the failing state, so it prescribes the cure for the case they imagined; when a guard hands you a fix, ask what OTHER world produces the identical red, because the remedy text cannot tell you and following it blindly can undo a decision that was correctly made.**

⏳ **F-2610-1 — A LANE's `DIRTY` BANNER ANSWERS "WHO OWNS THIS?" AND A FIRE ON A DRY BOARD IS ASKING "WHAT WOULD A REFILL LOSE?" — MEASURED ON lane-d: NOTHING, ALL 26 ALREADY ON ORIGIN (fire s2610, dry board, 78th consecutive; CODEX-WALL standing, owner POLICY, read AT SOURCE).** `lane-usable --all` prints `lane-d  art/portraits-e5-e10-generated  ahead=0  behind=654  tracked-dirt=26` and closes with `=> DIRTY: TRACKED dirt in the worktree — find its owner before touching this lane`. That is a CONTENTION verdict and it is CORRECT; what it does not say — and what F-2569-3 already names as the priced, unclaimed cure — is whether anything would actually be LOST, which is the question a fire reading it on a dry board is actually asking. 📊 **MEASURED s2610, controls asserted FIRST (F-2215-1 — `123,270` remote-reachable objects resolved and `hashed 26 of 26` files, so a "not found" would be an ANSWER and not a failed read): all `26` are evidence-prefixed (`reviews/shots-ss-06-e5-beats/**`), hashed FROM MAIN because a linked worktree SHARES main's object database, and they classify `SAFE 26 · LOCAL-REF-ONLY 0 · UNREFERENCED 0 · AT RISK 0`. Every byte is reachable from an origin ref. A `reset --hard` there would cost nothing.** ✅ **CORROBORATED AGAINST THE OTHER INSTRUMENT RATHER THAN ASSUMED, because `0 FACTORY-SIDE` is exactly the shape a PREDICATE MISS also prints: `isFactorySide` at `modified-tracked-evidence-census.mjs:94` matches `^worktrees/lane-[a-d]$` — READ, not grepped — so lane-d IS recognised as factory-side; and lane-d is among the `103` ANSWERED trees, not the `24` could-not-answer, established by running `git status --porcelain -uno` in it myself and getting 26 rows. So the census's `0 FACTORY-SIDE` is a real read about this lane, and the two instruments agree.** ⚖️ **SEVERITY STATED HONESTLY AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED — THIS IS A NEGATIVE RESULT, NOT A DEFECT: realised cost ZERO, nothing is at risk, nothing is broken, NOT a false green, and neither instrument was ever wrong. It is banked because F-2214-1 makes a negative inherit a measurement's duties, and because the pull to re-take it recurs on every dry board — the DIRTY banner prints in `--all` every single fire.** 🎯 **What earns it a row is that the two instruments AGREE and NEITHER SAYS SO ABOUT THIS LANE: `lane-usable` reports the dirt and has no opinion on durability, the census reports durability and names no lane. A fire reading `find its owner` spends budget hunting an owner for bytes that are already on origin, or files a durability hole that does not exist — the FALSE URGENCY class F-2566-1/F-2570-1/F-2571-1 each name on a different bucket, here arriving through a banner rather than a bucket.** ⚠️ **ONE METHOD NOTE PAID FOR IN THIS FIRE, AND IT IS THE HALF WORTH CARRYING: my first probe of the census's JSON asked `j.couldNotAnswer` and printed `couldNotAnswer count: 0` — AN ABSENT KEY. The real key is `treesCouldNotAnswer`, and it is a **NUMBER (`24`), not an array**, so a membership test on it is meaningless too. That is F-2295-1's class — a corpus going empty from a NAME — landing inside the very check I had written to avoid inferring, and it would have certified "lane-d was answered" on evidence of nothing. It was caught ONLY because I printed `Object.keys(j)` in the same command before reading any field. ➡️ **When you interrogate a JSON contract, print its KEYS before you read one, and check the TYPE before you test membership — a guessed field name returns `undefined`, and `undefined` prints as the reassuring answer.**** 🚫 **NO GUARD, and the restraint is INHERITED rather than re-argued: tracked dirt in a lane worktree is a LAWFUL, routine state — it is what a screenshot suite legitimately leaves behind — so a red would fire during ordinary correct operation and be excused into uselessness inside a week (F-1460-1, the `cross-engine` fate), which is the restraint F-2569-1 took for this very population. The priced cure remains F-2569-3 (add the durability read to `lane-usable`'s DIRTY branch), still deliberately unclaimed because that file carries a `--cure` path running `git reset --hard main` and needs behaviour-neutrality proof plus its own guard, not a drive-by.** 💡 **REUSABLE: the same 26 files answer two different questions — WHO may touch them (contention) and WHAT would be lost (durability) — and only the second decides whether a refill is safe to want. An instrument that answers one is not silent about the other by oversight; it is silent BY CONSTRUCTION, and a reader who takes its verdict as the answer to their own question is reading a true sentence about a subject they did not ask about.** ⏱️ **CLOSING BATTERY (s2610, F-1300-4 — run AFTER the handoff commit, routed through `node` because the npm form is gate-refused, F-2359-1): `npm run test:ledger-guards` GREEN, rc=0, **1159 assertions pass / 0 fail** + **83** bash legs, wall **177.9 s** (inside the F-2570-2 `~160 s` quiet band). The FIRST run reddened exactly TWO arms and both were MY OWN predicted pointer re-base — `NEW POINTER scripts/fire.md -> gazette-queue.md:1944` plus the `declaration prints on the happy path` arm that reds as its CONSEQUENCE, not independently (F-2590-2). Cured by `law-pointer-guard --update` ONLY AFTER eye-verifying line 1944 by reading it back, and the diff was checked to be MY ENTRY ALONE — `1942 -> 1944`, fingerprint `d7293afa016e` UNCHANGED, 2 insertions / 2 deletions, zero drift elsewhere — because `--update` rebuilds the WHOLE baseline from current state and would otherwise bless every drifted pointer at once (F-2595-1).** ⓘ **A green run of this battery prints `Killed: 9 … IGNORE_TERM=1 bash "$isolated/lane-runner-v3.sh"` on stderr: that is `runner-restart-recipe`s own teeth SIGKILLing an ISOLATED SCRATCH COPY, not your live runner (confirmed ALIVE this fire). Do NOT reach for §2.0b on it.** ⚠️ **AND A SECOND METHOD NOTE OF THE SAME CLASS AS THIS ROW's FIRST: my aggregation needle keyed on `^# tests` and returned `{