 it as its first act and caught it in one step, and line-1 read `lock CLEARED`, so §1.1's liveness branch was never entered and no lock was misjudged.** 🎯 **WHAT EARNS IT A ROW IS THE DIRECTION AND THE SILENCE: because the eaten pattern is *well-formed*, grep reports the no-match **VERDICT** — `rc=1` with empty stderr, which is **precisely the discriminator F-2212-1 recommends for distinguishing a verdict from a crash**, here certifying a MALFORMED PATTERN as a true zero. A zero reads as "no `FIRE END` found" → "the owner may still be live" → **STAND DOWN**: the silent, self-perpetuating ~38-minute stall §1.1 exists to prevent. And the route is not exotic — §2.0d prescribes `node -e "…"` for every gate-refused command, and a fire batching several probes into one call reaches for it by habit, which is exactly how s2604 arrived.** ➡️ **CURE, TYPED AND RUN BEFORE IT WAS LANDED (F-2360-1's rule): use a FIXED-STRING anchor, which has no metacharacters and therefore no escaping depth — `grep -F '[fire-runner] ' logs/fire-$(date +%Y%m%d).log | grep -cF 'FIRE END'` → **8** bare, and **8** through the identical node wrapper that broke the regex form. Landed at the site in `scripts/fire.md` §1.1, beside F-2295-1.** 🚫 **NO GUARD, and the restraint is INHERITED rather than re-argued: "did a fire escape a regex correctly in an ad-hoc probe?" is unobservable to every battery — the same reason F-1669-1 declined one for a filename read with the eyes, F-2391-1 and F-2562-2 for hand-typed date windows, F-2567-1 for a hand-typed clock, and F-2599-1 for a `| tail`. The cure is the sentence at the site.** 💡 **REUSABLE, AND IT IS THE NEAREST SIBLING OF F-2295-1, WHICH SITS TWO SENTENCES ABOVE IT IN THE SAME CLAUSE: that finding showed a corpus can go empty from a **NAME**. This one shows it can go empty from an **ESCAPING LAYER** — and the layer is introduced by this law's OWN prescribed fallback, so the pattern is correct on the page and wrong at the process. This streak has asked many times how a subject set can come back empty (a swallow, a guard, a `catch`, a cwd, a stale tree, a filename, a timezone, a registry, a clock, a truncation); **a quoting depth is a new one, and unlike the others it is introduced by the cure rather than by the subject.** When a law quotes a REGEX, the quoting depth between the page and the tool is part of the instruction — prefer a FIXED STRING for any anchor, because a fixed string has no depth.** ⚠️ **TWO METHOD NOTES PAID FOR IN THIS FIRE, both caught by controls rather than by reading. (1) My probe harness CRASHED TWICE on nested double quotes, and the SECOND crash still yielded the evidence, because bash echoed the collapsed program back verbatim — `'^[fire-runner] .*FIRE END'`, backslashes gone. **A crashing probe CAN be a measurement when its error text contains the subject; do NOT score that as a passing control** (the construction-refusal rule — a variant that cannot parse is evidence about construction, not about the defect). (2) **MY OWN CLAUSE SHIPPED THE DEFECT IT DESCRIBES, one layer up:** the first splice rendered `\[` as `\\[` because my template literal took its own bite, so a clause whose entire subject is backslash arithmetic printed the wrong backslash count. Caught by reading the landed bytes back rather than trusting the write — which is the same eye-check this law demands of every re-based pointer, applied to prose.**

⏳ **F-2603-1 — THE OWNERSHIP PREDICATE MISSES EVERY FIRE *GATE* WORKTREE SHAPE BUT ONE, AND THE ONE IT MATCHES IS FIVE EMPTY CORPSES (fire s2603, dry board, 71st consecutive; CODEX-WALL standing, owner POLICY).** s2602 widened `isFactorySide()`'s APPLICATION from one bucket to three; nobody re-examined the PREDICATE, which is F-2358-1's own lesson (*the selector nobody questioned is the one the author never had to defend*). 📊 MEASURED s2603 over all `127` registered worktrees, control asserted first (F-2215-1 — the registry returned 127, so a zero would be an ANSWER): `9` trees carry the fire-gate shape (an `s<NNN>` session token + `gate`), and the predicate classified **`5` FACTORY / `4` attended**. 🪦 **ALL FIVE IT MATCHES (`/tmp/gr-gate-s25NN`) ARE GIT-PRUNABLE CORPSES HOLDING ZERO REGULAR FILES** — measured gitlessly per F-2485-1, one of them across `2,557` directories, so the zero is an ANSWER and not a failed read. **The ONE readable gate tree is one it MISSES:** `<repo>/gate-s2501`, detached, `26,757` tracked files, holding `6` evidence-prefixed modified-tracked files (`reviews/shots-f1742-1/*.png`) — **precisely the `gate-s2501` 6 that F-2569-1 records salvaging as FACTORY-SIDE.** So the mechanised predicate contradicts, by name, the hand judgement it was built to encode. 🧭 **AND IT MATCHES THE SHAPE A FIRE CANNOT GATE IN:** the standing fire memory prescribes `worktrees/gate-s<N>` **inside the repo root** and records `/tmp` as unusable — *"every later `cd /tmp/gate-s1391 && npx …` is refused … Playwright and vite both need cwd"* — so the only out-of-root rule named the deprecated shape while neither in-root shape matched anything. ⚖️ **SEVERITY STATED HONESTLY AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED: LATENT, realised cost ZERO, and NOT a false green — `FACTORY-SIDE 0` was TRUE in every bucket, because all `6` of gate-s2501's files are `SAFE` (verified: `6/6` origin-reachable, controls `123,095` remote / `185,125` all-ref). They are safe precisely BECAUSE s2569 salvaged them by hand before this predicate existed — the mechanisation's only live subject is clean because a human-judged act already discharged it.** 🎯 **What earns it a cure is the DIRECTION and a reachability that is the PRESCRIBED CASE, not an edge case: §3.0b MANDATES gating undecided content in a detached worktree and §4.1 mandates screenshots into `reviews/shots-*/` — an evidence prefix — so a fire-owned gate tree holding evidence-prefixed files is the ORDINARY OUTCOME OF EVERY DRAIN.** The moment the board stops being dry, a fire's OWN gate evidence is attributed to the owner (*REPORT, never touch* — F-2561-1) and lands on a desk already `7` items deep, instead of being salvaged by the fire that owns it — which is the act F-2569-1 already established as correct. 🛠️ **CURE: two named patterns — `^(worktrees/)?gate-s\d+$` in-root and `/tmp/gr-(gate-s\d+|s\d+-[a-z-]*gate)` out-of-root. UNRECOGNISED STILL MEANS ATTENDED, deliberately: that is the FAIL-SAFE direction, because a false *attended* costs a report while a false *factory* invites a fire to write in someone else's tree (Mistake #2). The `s<NNN>` token is load-bearing — measured, `0` of the 127 attended trees carry it.** ✅ **BEHAVIOUR-NEUTRAL BEFORE LANDING (F-1274-2): census, `--json` and the sibling importer `untracked-evidence-durability.mjs` all **3/3 BYTE-IDENTICAL**, rc unchanged, each control arm asserted to have PRODUCED output before any "identical" was believed. It changes no verdict today; it changes what happens the next time a gate tree holds undurable evidence.** 🔒 **Guarded by arm 9b of the EXISTING `modified-tracked-evidence-census-guard.test.mjs` — no new file (F-1261-1, and a second guard here is the sprawl this repo warns about); 16 arms, `3.0 s`, already rooted in `test:ledger-guards`.** 🔬 **TEETH, 5 variants on scratch copies, subject restored BYTE-IDENTICAL (sha256-asserted) after every one: PRE-CURE verbatim reds 9b · **BOTH over-general directions red 9b** (match-any-`gate`, and drop-the-session-token — caught by the reverse controls inside the arm) · in-root-only reds 9b · and the TOTAL-FAILURE mutation reds all `17`, so no arm is vacuous.** ⚠️ **ONE METHOD NOTE PAID FOR IN THIS FIRE, AND IT IS F-2215-1 BITING ITS OWN CITER: my gitless walk printed `files=0` for all 8 trees UNDER A CONTROL SENTENCE THAT READ *"the walk above proves the trees were READ (non-zero file counts)"* — and the counts were ZERO. The verdict was right (the leaves are vendor `openclaw/*` SYMLINKS, which the walk correctly skips) and the justification was false. I caught it only because `0` looked surprising, not because any assertion fired. **A control that asserts its own validity can assert it WRONGLY; read what the control actually printed, not what you wrote it to print.**** 💡 **REUSABLE, and it is a NEW AXIS after *how WIDE is the selector* (F-2358-1) and *can its axis EXPRESS the defect* (F-2389-1): THIS SELECTOR'S AXIS IS RIGHT AND ITS ENUMERATION IS FROZEN AT THE SHAPES THAT EXISTED WHEN IT WAS WRITTEN. A path convention is not a constant — it drifts as the factory changes where it works — so a predicate keyed on one is a census with an expiry date nobody records. When you mechanise a judgement over paths, LIST THE LIVE INSTANCES AND CHECK THE PREDICATE AGAINST ALL OF THEM; here that was one command and it found 4 misses in 9.**

⏳ **F-2602-1 — THE MODIFIED-TRACKED CENSUS ATTRIBUTES ONLY THE DESK FIGURE, SO ITS LARGEST NON-SAFE PILE HAS NO OWNER PRINTED (fire s2602, dry board, 70th consecutive; CODEX-WALL standing, owner POLICY).** `modified-tracked-evidence-census.mjs` prints ALL FOUR buckets "so the predicate is visible" (F-2560-1) and applied `isFactorySide()` to the **AT RISK bucket ALONE** (`:190`, verified by READING). 📊 MEASURED s2602 over the whole registry, controls asserted first (F-2215-1 — `35218` tracked · `123071` remote-reachable · `185101` all-ref-reachable, `127` registered / `103` answered / `24` could-not-answer, `1589` subjects, so a zero is an ANSWER): `LOCAL-REF-ONLY` is **`1048` files / `1350.9 MB` — 3.0x the desk figure — and carried NO ownership read at all**. Ownership is the field that decides whether a fire may TOUCH a tree (F-2561-1), so a reader met a four-figure pile it could not attribute. ⚖️ **SEVERITY HONEST AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED: LATENT, realised cost ZERO, NOT a false green — FACTORY-SIDE measured `0` in ALL THREE non-SAFE buckets, so nothing was ever mis-attributed and every verdict this tool published was TRUE.** 🎯 The DIRECTION earns it: a fire meeting an unattributed pile either ignores it or reaches into an attended tree, which is the Mistake #2 direction F-2489-1 forbids in the same breath. ✅ **THREE NEGATIVES BANKED WITH THEIR MECHANISM (F-2214-1) SO NOBODY RE-TAKES THEM: (1) the desk row F-2569-2 REPRODUCES EXACTLY — `480` files / `447.9 MB` / `24` trees, byte-identical to s2596, `0` FACTORY-SIDE; (2) `LOCAL-REF-ONLY` is likewise **`0` FACTORY-SIDE**, `1041` of its `1048` files sitting in ONE attended tree (`~/.codex/worktrees/5b60/Gold Rush`), so the whole 1350.9 MB is an OWNER matter and NO fire owes anything there; (3) **lane-d reads `DIRTY` with `26` tracked-dirt files and ALL 26 ARE `SAFE` ON ORIGIN** — so the one factory lane flagged DIRTY today would lose NOTHING to a refresh, which is the question F-2569-3 names as priced-but-unclaimed.** 🛠️ CURE: every non-SAFE bucket now carries its own FACTORY-SIDE/attended split, named trees, printed ALWAYS including the all-clear (F-2208-1), mirrored in `--json` as `ownershipByBucket`; the exit code still keys on FACTORY-SIDE **AT RISK** alone, so no new teeth. A caveat at the site states that a non-SAFE verdict is not by itself an owed act, because a retention transform may hold the content in another SHAPE that blob identity cannot see (F-2570-1/F-2571-1). ✅ BEHAVIOUR-NEUTRAL BEFORE LANDING (F-1274-2): `0` lines removed, `7` added, rc unchanged in all three modes, no pre-existing `--json` key altered; the control asserted its own validity first (each arm logged `2039 B` before any diff was believed). 🔒 Guarded by `scripts/census-bucket-ownership-guard.test.mjs` — 10 arms, **1.9 s**, rooted in `test:ledger-guards`, `gate-caller-audit` PASS. 🧪 **IT RUNS ON A FIXTURE AND THE REASON IS MEASURED, NOT STYLISTIC: live it is `72 s` (127 worktrees, three spawns) and F-2159-1 prices a >=30 s battery addition as OWNER policy rather than a fire's drive-by; and FACTORY-SIDE is `0` on the live board, so a live guard could ONLY EVER OBSERVE THE ALL-CLEAR — asserting the principle exactly where it holds and never where it fails, which is F-2208-1's own blind spot. The fixture manufactures a factory-side row in AT RISK *and* in LOCAL-REF-ONLY.** 🔬 **TEETH, 8 variants on scratch copies with the subject restored BYTE-IDENTICAL (sha256-asserted) after every one, and the sweep doubled as a reachability audit (s2226): ALL 10 ARMS red under at least one variant, so none is decoration — pre-cure verbatim reds 8 · declare-only-on-failure reds 2 · narrow-to-AT-RISK reds 3 · drop-the-retention-caveat reds 1 ALONE · re-implement-the-predicate reds 1 ALONE · drop a pre-existing json key reds the behaviour-neutrality arm ALONE · a total subject failure reds the fixture-validity control.** ⚠️ **THE METHOD NOTE IS WORTH MORE THAN THE CURE, AND IT IS s2601'S LESSON ARRIVING FROM THE OTHER SIDE: MY REVERSE CONTROL — the arm I was surest of, the one guarding against giving an advisory new teeth — REDDENED **NOTHING**, AND READ AS A PASS.** My fixture carried factory bytes in BOTH AT RISK and LOCAL-REF-ONLY, and on such a fixture the correct rule (`--strict` keys on AT RISK) and the over-general rule (`--strict` keys on any non-SAFE factory bytes) **AGREE ON EVERY INPUT** — both exit 1. The arm was not weak, it was UNAIMED: I had written in its own comment that the fixture "can tell the two rules apart", which was false when written. It took a SECOND fixture — factory bytes in LOCAL-REF-ONLY and NONE in AT RISK — for the two rules to diverge (correct → rc 0, over-general → rc 1), and the arm then reds under that variant ALONE. 💡 **REUSABLE, and it is a NEW AXIS after *how WIDE is the selector* (F-2358-1), *can its axis EXPRESS the defect* (F-2389-1), *which TREE* (F-2484-1), *a registry is not a census* (F-2485-1), *entries vs files* (F-2490-1) and *an undeclared denominator* (F-2561-1) — every one of those interrogates the SUBJECT SET or the PREDICATE. **THIS ONE IS ABOUT THE FIXTURE A CONTROL RUNS ON: two rules that differ can be OBSERVATIONALLY IDENTICAL on the case you happened to build, so a reverse control is only as sharp as the DIVERGENCE its fixture admits.** Before trusting a control that distinguishes two rules, ask what input makes them disagree — and if you cannot name one, the control is measuring nothing and will say so only when someone manufactures the defect.** ⚠️ **AND A SECOND, CHEAPER TRAP IN THE SWEEP ITSELF: my teeth harness keyed "green" on `# fail 0` where `node --test` prints `ℹ fail 0`, so the BASELINE read as red and the harness refused. It failed LOUD and cost a minute — but a matcher that cannot parse its own runner is the s2601 under-reporting defect one notch over, and it now REFUSES when no fail-count line is found rather than inferring.** 🚫 **NO BOARD GUARD, and the restraint is INHERITED rather than re-argued: modified tracked evidence in an attended tree is a LAWFUL, routine state, so a red would fire during ordinary correct operation and be excused into uselessness inside a week (F-1460-1, the `cross-engine` fate) — the same restraint F-2357-1 through F-2585-1 all took for this sweep. What IS guarded is our own instrument.** [s2602 · cure `967464a4e`]

⏳ **F-2601-1 — THE LB-01 SUPPLY THRESHOLD IS *ONE DAY'S DICE ROLL*, NOT A SCHEDULE: THE DROPLET'S TIMER CARRIES `RandomizedDelaySec=1h`, SO THE TRIGGER IS UNIFORM IN [02:00, 03:00) UTC AND RE-ROLLED DAILY, AND F-2600-1'S `~02:10 UTC` IS WRONG IN BOTH DIRECTIONS (fire s2601, dry board, 69th consecutive; CODEX-WALL standing).** F-2600-1 landed ONE FIRE EARLIER and I am its first executor: I ran the pull at `01:52 UTC`, inside its own stated futile window, and the cure FIRED CORRECTLY in the wild — `⏳ NOT DISCHARGED for today — ledger-2026-09-17.db does not exist on the box yet`. **Its diagnosis is right and its NUMBER is not.** 📊 MEASURED by reading the UNIT rather than its next firing, control asserted first (F-2215-1 — the box answered `2026-09-17T01:55:31Z` / `Etc/UTC`): `OnCalendar=*-*-* 02:00:00 UTC` + **`RandomizedDelaySec=1h`** + `Persistent=true`. CORROBORATED from the box's own file mtimes across all `15` backups it holds (control: 15 rows returned): writes land **`02:07:02`–`02:57:58` UTC, mean `34.5` min past 02:00**. 🎯 So the threshold fails twice: on `2026-09-04` the mirror existed at `02:07:02` and a fire obeying *"do not run before ~02:10"* would have declined a pull that WOULD have worked (the direction this duty's purpose cares about); on `2026-09-15` it arrived at `02:57:58`, so inferring the complement burns ~10 more futile cycles. ⚖️ SEVERITY HONEST AND NOT INFLATED: realised cost **ZERO**, nothing lost, NOT a false green, `ledger-mirror-freshness` reads `WHOLE AND CURRENT 24/24`; and the SITE message was already closer to right than the law sentence (`~02:10–03:00`), so the defect is in the LAW's binding number, not the tool. ➡️ CURE ABOLISHES the threshold rather than moving it: after `02:00 UTC` just RUN THE PULL — idempotent, bounded (**3.0 s** measured), self-declaring — and read its verdict. 🔒 Site restated + a 6th guard arm (`ledger-pull-supply-window-guard.test.mjs`) asserting the site names `RandomizedDelaySec` and states a RANGE, since restoring a threshold is silent in every other channel (F-1667-1). 🔬 TEETH, 6 variants IN PLACE, subject restored BYTE-IDENTICAL (sha256-asserted): the new arm reds under ALL FIVE defect variants; the REVERSE CONTROL reds s2600's arms 2+5 ALONE. ⚠️ And I VERIFIED s2600's own reachability claims rather than inherit them (Mistake #4 governs reassurance too): day-name→arm 1 ALONE ✓, exit-1→arm 3 ✓, over-general→REVERSE CONTROL ALONE ✓. All 6 arms reachable. ⓘ METHOD NOTE: my first sweep labelled three of s2600's arms `*** DECORATION ***` — they are not; **a reachability audit's denominator is the VARIANT SET YOU BUILT, not the arm set it prints**, so it accuses a previous author's arms by default. (It also double-counted: `node --test` prints each failure twice.) 💡 REUSABLE, a NEW AXIS: this streak has asked how a measurement can be WRONG, and F-2492-1 added RIGHT-AND-EXPIRED. **This one is RIGHT AND NOT REPEATABLE — a draw from a distribution banked as a constant.** `systemctl list-timers` prints a `NEXT` column: one concrete, authoritative-looking timestamp that the daemon RE-ROLLS DAILY, while the UNIT file is where the distribution lives. **When you ask a machine for its schedule, ask whether the schedule is DETERMINISTIC — read the definition, not the next firing — and record whether a banked timing constant is a PROPERTY or a SAMPLE, because the two are typographically identical and only one survives tomorrow.**

⏳ **F-2600-1 — A DUTY BECOMES *OWED* HOURS BEFORE IT BECOMES *SATISFIABLE*, AND LB-01 NAMED ONLY THE FIRST CLOCK: THE MIRROR FOR UTC-DAY D CANNOT EXIST UNTIL THE DROPLET'S OWN TIMER FIRES AT ~02:10 UTC, SO EVERY PULL IN THE 00:00–02:10 UTC WINDOW IS A GUARANTEED NO-OP (fire s2600, dry board, 68th consecutive).** 📊 **MEASURED ON THE BOX rather than inferred, with the CONTROL asserted first (F-2215-1 — the droplet answered `2026-09-17T00:35:24Z`, `TZ=Etc/UTC`, so a blank would have been a failed read and not an answer): there is NO crontab (`no crontab for root`); the writer is the systemd unit **`goldrush-ledger-backup.timer`**, `NEXT` = `02:08:48 UTC`, and its last five real writes are `02:26:44 · 02:45:08 · 02:50:09 · 02:57:58 · 02:28:44 UTC`. CORROBORATED INDEPENDENTLY FROM OUR OWN SIDE — the git add-commit times of all `24` mirrors put every on-time pull at `02:36–04:10 UTC` (n=14, excluding the 08-24 bootstrap at 00:34 and three backfill batches). Two methods, different data, same window.** ⚙️ **BOTH SIDES NAME THE FILE IN UTC (`ledger-backup-pull.mjs:10`, `ops/droplet/ledger-backup.mjs:43`), so at UTC midnight today's mirror is instantly OWED while the supply is ~2h away — `07:00`–`09:10` local (UTC+07), about twenty futile fire-cycles at the five-minute cadence, each ssh-ing the owner's box.** ⛔ **AND THE NO-OP IS EASY TO RECORD AS A DISCHARGE, WHICH IS THE HALF THAT EARNS A ROW: the script has TWO "nothing to do" exits and only ONE is a discharge. `:34` says *"ledger backup already present **for today**"* — discharged. `:49` says *"newest ledger backup already present"* and names **YESTERDAY'S** file — NOT discharged. And `:49` is not merely *usually* about an outstanding day, it is **PROVABLY ALWAYS** so: reaching it means today's mirror is absent locally (the `:33` guard) while the box's newest IS present locally, so the box's newest cannot be today's. The conditional I first drafted for the cure was dead code, and that is what makes the old message's silence worse than it looks.** ✅ **CURED AT THE SITE so it cannot rot out of sync with the law sentence: `:49` now prints `⏳ NOT DISCHARGED for today — <name> does not exist on the box yet`, names the timer and the window, and says *"nothing is wrong"* — a supply gap is not a fetch failure. **Exit code UNCHANGED at 0**; LB-01's "box-side gaps are a WARN, never a red" is untouched, and the sibling guards `ledger-backup-pull.test.mjs` (2/2) and `ledger-backup-fill-gaps-guard` (8/8) stay green.** ⚖️ **SEVERITY STATED HONESTLY AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED: realised cost ZERO, nothing was lost, NOT a false green, and NO MIRROR HAS EVER BEEN MISSED FOR THIS REASON — `ledger-mirror-freshness` reads `WHOLE AND CURRENT, 24/24 days present`. The duty's own discharge test is the safety net and it works: today's file is genuinely absent, so the next fire re-runs the pull and gets it once the timer has fired.** 🎯 **What earns it a row is the DIRECTION at the one moment it fires: s2599's next-fire priority (A) read *"after ~07:00 local, re-run `ledger-backup-pull.mjs` — that is when UTC rolls to 09-17 and the 09-17 mirror becomes owed"*, which is TRUE about owed-ness and names the wrong clock. s2600 obeyed it at `07:30` local = `00:30` UTC, **1h38m before the timer**, and a fire that then wrote "LB-01 discharged" off the word *present* would have published a false discharge into the handoff chain.** 🚫 **NO BOARD GUARD, and the restraint is INHERITED rather than re-argued: a box that has not yet run its nightly timer is a LAWFUL, routine, every-single-day state, so a red on "today's mirror is missing" would fire during ordinary correct operation and be excused into uselessness inside a week (F-1460-1, the `cross-engine` fate) — the same restraint F-2351-1 took in shipping the freshness probe advisory at exit 0. What IS guarded is OUR OWN CODE: `scripts/ledger-pull-supply-window-guard.test.mjs`, 5 arms, rooted in `test:ledger-guards`, `gate-caller-audit` PASS.** 🔬 **TEETH, 7 variants manufactured IN PLACE with the subject restored BYTE-IDENTICAL (sha256-asserted) after every one: ALL 5 ARMS red under at least one variant, so none is decoration — pre-cure verbatim reds 1,2,5 · the OVER-GENERAL cure (declare on the genuine-discharge exit too) reds the REVERSE CONTROL **ALONE** · a declaration omitting the day's NAME reds arm 1 **ALONE** · dropping the timer from stdout while keeping it in the comment reds arm 2 **ALONE**, which is what proves arm 2 reads OUTPUT and not source · a no-op that exits 1 reds arm 3.** ⚠️ **ONE PREDICTION CORRECTED, AND IT IS THE METHOD NOTE WORTH MORE THAN THE CURE: my provenance-stripping variant reddened **NOTHING**, and my first instinct was to write arm 5 off as decoration. The VARIANT was under-powered, not the arm — `measured s2600` appears TWICE at the site (once in the comment, once in the printed line) and I had stripped only one copy. Stripping both reds arm 5 **ALONE**. **A variant that leaves a second copy of its target standing is not evidence about the arm; count your needle's occurrences before you call an assertion toothless.**** 💡 **REUSABLE, and it is a NEW AXIS after *how WIDE is the selector* (F-2358-1), *can its axis EXPRESS the defect* (F-2389-1), *which TREE* (F-2484-1), *a registry is not a census* (F-2485-1), *an undeclared denominator* (F-2561-1) and *which CLOCK* (F-2567-1): every one of those interrogates a MEASUREMENT. **This one is about a DUTY, and a duty can have TWO schedules — the one that makes it OWED and the one that makes it SUPPLYABLE — set by different machines and hours apart.** A handoff that names only the first manufactures guaranteed-futile work and invites a false discharge. When a duty waits on something a machine you do not run must produce first, ASK THAT MACHINE FOR ITS SCHEDULE: one `systemctl list-timers` refuted a timing premise two fires had been reasoning from, which is §2.0b's lesson (*a predecessor's stated reason is a CLAIM, not a constraint*) applied to a clock i