rstatement published as a measurement. F-2182-1 governs your own prose exactly as it governs an inherited claim.** 🚫 **NO GUARD, and the restraint is INHERITED rather than re-argued: "did a fire pipe this through `tail`?" is unobservable to every battery — the same reason F-1669-1 declined one for a filename read with the eyes, F-2391-1 and F-2562-2 for hand-typed date windows, and F-2567-1 for a hand-typed clock.** 🔺 **F-2599-2, PRICED AND UNCLAIMED so the next fire inherits a job and not a hunch: re-print the budget block and the three counts as a CLOSING summary, which is what BOTH sibling instruments already do and which retires the hazard for every future reader whatever `N` they pick. DELIBERATELY NOT DONE HERE — the tool costs `321.9 s` per run (measured, above), so an end-to-end CLI arm cannot be rooted in a mandated battery at F-2159-1's own gate-policy precedent — **that precedent prices a >=30 s addition as OWNER policy, and this is 10x it, so the conclusion survives the corrected figure rather than resting on it** — and testing the emitter in isolation while the call site stays unexercised is F-2209-1 exactly. It needs a measured plan, not a drive-by.** 💡 **REUSABLE, a NEW AXIS after *how WIDE is the selector* (F-2358-1), *can its axis EXPRESS the defect* (F-2389-1), *which TREE* (F-2484-1), *a registry is not a census* (F-2485-1), *entries vs files* (F-2490-1) and *an undeclared denominator* (F-2561-1) — every one of those interrogates the MEASUREMENT. THIS ONE IS ABOUT THE TRUNCATION BETWEEN A CORRECT TOOL AND ITS READER. An instrument's verdict has a POSITION as well as a value, and a reader who must truncate is making a silent bet on that position. When a law tells a fire to READ a line, say WHERE the line is — and when you add a line a law will cite, put it where the other instruments put theirs.**

