curate as written and needs no correction. What is recorded here is a READING HAZARD in front of a dated trigger.** 🎯 **What earns it a row is the DIRECTION at the one moment it fires: F-2568-1 is read by a fire holding a mint on 2026-09-21. A fire that greps `rotation_closed`, lands on `:942`, and concludes the law misdescribes the code has exactly one obvious "cure" — add the window test to `:492` — and that cure would 403 every closed rotation’s board, breaking a published contract on a rider-facing surface, with a green battery attesting to it (no test asserts the read path’s permissiveness).** ⛔ **OVER-CORRECTION FENCE, which is the half that matters more than the finding: DO NOT add a window check to the transfer-board READ path. If a future ruling ever wants closed boards hidden, that is an owner call against `skill.md:367`’s published sentence, not a consistency fix.** ✅ **THREE RT-01 PRE-FLIGHT FACTS BANKED IN PASSING, four days ahead of the trigger, because each is cheap and each fails LATE if unchecked: (1) the owner-held salt EXISTS — `~/.goldrush/rotation-salt`, 65 B, mode `600` — so the law’s *"a missing salt file is a STOP with one desk line, never a default"* arm is NOT armed; (2) `site/assay-office.js:2` reads `CURRENT_ROTATION_ID = 'r2026w38'`, which is BOTH the OPEN rotation (`opensAt` 2026-09-14, `closesAt` 2026-09-21) and the NEWEST minted, so the RT-01 law (OPEN) and `skillmd-guard` (NEWEST) AGREE today and F-2568-1’s jointly-unsatisfiable window is genuinely shut until Sunday; (3) F-2568-2’s desk premise is re-verified ACCURATE rather than inherited — `currentOrLatestRotation` (`:1188–1191`) already filters `opensAt <= now`, sorts descending and takes the first, so the open rotation IS derivable in the same file that re-states it by hand.** 🚫 **NO GUARD, and the restraint is INHERITED rather than re-argued: *"does a prose citation still describe the operation it names?"* is a JUDGEMENT over two files, so a red would fire on every honest citation and be excused into uselessness inside a week (F-1460-1, the `cross-engine` fate) — the same restraint F-2450-1 took for a live instruction and F-2492-1 for an expired convention. The cure is the sentence you are reading, sitting beside the clause it protects.** 💡 **REUSABLE, and it is a NEW AXIS for this streak: twenty-odd findings have asked how a MEASUREMENT can be wrong — a swallow, a guard, a cwd, a filename, a timezone, a registry, a clock, an expired census. THIS ONE IS RIGHT, AND READS AS WRONG. Two sentences can name the same noun, cite the same file, and contradict each other flatly while both being exact, because they govern different OPERATIONS on it — and the cheap key (grep the error string) lands on the path you are NOT asking about. Before you reconcile two sources that disagree, ask which OPERATION each one governs; and when the reconciliation would change player-facing behaviour, that is not a tidy-up, it is a ruling.**

