🔺 **F-2586-2 — A CONCURRENT *ATTENDED* HANDOFF DROPPED THREE OPEN OWNER-DESK ITEMS AND A FIRE'S OWN LINE-1, AND THE ONLY THING THAT CAUGHT IT WAS RE-RUNNING THE CLOSING BATTERY (measured and recovered s2586).** At 08:24 the attended session landed the playability first wave; at 08:29 it rewrote `STATUS.md` line 1 with its own handoff. Two things went with it. **(1) THE DESK LOST THREE ITEMS WITH NO REASON GIVEN** — `F-2569-2`, `F-2568-2`, `F-2563-2`, i.e. a `496.5 MB` retention question, the `CURRENT_ROTATION_ID` fork and a `19.86 GB` push decision — leaving a 4-item desk where the inherited one held 6. **(2) THE s2586 HANDOFF LINE-1 WAS DROPPED UNARCHIVED**, the §4 / F-2403-1 harm arriving from a hand the fire was not holding. ⚖️ **SEVERITY STATED HONESTLY AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED: realised cost ZERO, nothing is lost, and this is NOT a false green — `desk-carryforward-guard` reddened LOUDLY and correctly, and `STATUS.md` is TRACKED so the dropped line was recoverable verbatim from `ae68b3c32`. Nor is it an accusation: an attended session composing a fresh handoff after a long drain is doing ordinary correct work, and the three items are not ITS findings.** 🎯 **What earns it a row is WHEN it was caught. The desk gates SKIP while line 1 reads `ACTIVE` (F-2227-2), so they can only ever fire AFTER a handoff commit — and my battery had already run GREEN at 08:24 against MY line 1. The drop happened five minutes later, i.e. inside the one window in which no gate was watching, and I found it only because I re-ran the battery after a LATER ledger write. **A fire whose last act is its battery is protected; a fire whose battery is not its last act is not, and neither is the board between two writers.** ➡️ **RECOVERED, and the two halves were handled differently ON PURPOSE: the handoff line was restored VERBATIM from its commit (never rewrite another session's account of its own work), and the three desk items were carried back BYTE-VERBATIM from that same archived line,