✅ **F-2577-1 — THE ATTENDED SELF-UPDATE FALSE RED IS CURED, BY A ROUTE ITS OWN FINDING DID NOT CONSIDER: SUPERSESSION IS DECIDABLE FROM *CONTENT*, SO NO SESSION IDENTITY IS NEEDED AT ALL (measured and cured s2577).** F-2576-2 diagnosed this exactly and correctly — `status-archive-audit`’s same-session exclusion is keyed on an `sNNNN` token, an attended line carries none, both sides parse to `null`, the `!== null` guards drop the pair through, and the run prints `snull destroyed snull’s handoff line`. It then DECLINED the cure, and **its restraint was right**: collapsing every attended line into one identity would suppress a genuine destruction of one attended session’s handoff by a DIFFERENT attended session — trading a false RED for a false GREEN, in the population that now carries the drain load (F-1546-1). 🔑 **WHAT MOVED IS THE DECOMPOSITION, NOT THE RISK APPETITE. F-2576-2 wrote that a sound fix "needs a session identity an attended line actually carries (its own commit, or a stamp)" — that is the fix IT imagined, and it is a hard one. But the question the instrument is really asking is not *who wrote these two lines*, it is *did anything get destroyed*, and THAT is answerable from the lines themselves.** The newer line SUPERSEDES the older when it preserves every load-bearing token the older carried: every finding id, every commit hash (prefix-tolerant across the 8/9/40-char widths this repo mixes — F-1633-1), and every finding id named in the older line’s OWNER’S DESK segment. **That is precisely the check s2576 and s2577 each ran BY HAND before clearing the live instance; it is now mechanical.** 📊 **MEASURED on the live board, and the two lines differ by FOUR TOKENS out of 220 — the stamp, and the words "deploy this handoff" which the newer replaces with the deploy’s actual outcome. Every F-id, every hash and every desk item is preserved, verified by set-difference rather than by reading.** ⚖️ **THE ARM DECLARES AND DOES NOT CLEAR (the F-2366-1 restraint): the event is still reported, on its own `SUPERSEDED` line, with its commit subject, so a reader can overturn the judgement — and the COUNT rides in the always-printed verdict line, surviving `--quiet` (F-2208-1: a judgement nobody is shown cannot be re-judged).** 🎯 **BLAST RADIUS DELIBERATELY MINIMISED BY MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN BY TASTE, and this took three attempts: placed above the ABRIDGED test the arm reclassified `869f6cbc`, a pre-existing ABRIDGED entry; placed above the permanence split it reclassified `0180b013`, a pre-existing TRANSIENT. Both of those classes are already correct, already benign, and say something STRICTLY MORE SPECIFIC than "superseded". The arm now sits below both and touches ONLY pairs that would otherwise be reported PERMANENTLY LOST.** ✅ **DELTA MEASURED, NOT CLAIMED NEUTRAL (F-1274-2 governs the measurement, not the outcome): across 5 modes the ONLY change is the intended one — `DROPPED`/`LOST: 1`/rc=1 becomes `SUPERSEDED`/`CLEAN: 0`/rc=0. Transient 3, abridged 1, excluded 5, replacements 38, examined 40 are all byte-identical, and the misuse path still refuses at rc=2. Controls asserted their own validity first (F-2215-1).** 🔧 **AND IT CLEARS THE STANDING BATTERY RED s2576 REPORTED HONESTLY AND COULD NOT FIX: `test:ledger-guards` chains `node scripts/status-archive-audit.mjs --limit 40 --quiet` bare, so that leg was the rc=1 every fire was inheriting.** 🔒 **Guarded by `scripts/status-archive-supersession-guard.test.mjs` — 10 arms, 1.8 s, rooted in `test:ledger-guards`; `gate-caller-audit` PASS.** 🔬 **TEETH, 9 variants manufactured on scratch copies with the subject restored byte-identical and the restoration ASSERTED: every over-general cure is caught by exactly the reverse control built for it — **the blanket-exclude cure F-2576-2 warned against reds arm 2**, dropping the DESK test reds **3 ALONE**, exact-hash equality reds **5 ALONE**, re-ordering above ABRIDGED reds **7 ALONE**, dropping the count from the verdict reds **8 ALONE**, unscoping reds **6 and 10**, and deleting the pre-existing same-session exclusion reds **9 ALONE**.** ⚠️ **ONE METHOD NOTE, AND THE TEETH SWEEP IS WHAT CAUGHT IT: arm 9 was DECORATION as first written. It claimed to prove the cure is scoped to the attended pair — but unscoping is already caught by arm 6, because a fire’s handoff ROUTINELY carries its predecessor’s desk and findings forward and therefore satisfies the supersession predicate. Nine variants reddened it with nothing. It was retargeted to the defect that does reach it (deleting the original same-session exclusion, on the theory that the new arm subsumes it — it does not). **An arm named for a defence it cannot provide is worse than no arm: it makes the next reader stop looking.**** 💡 **REUSABLE, and it is the §2.0b lesson on a new subject: A PREDECESSOR’S STATED REASON FOR DECLINING IS A CLAIM WITH A PRICE ON IT, AND SO IS THE SHOPPING LIST THEY ATTACH TO IT. F-2576-2’s "a sound fix needs X, Y and Z" describes the fix its author could see; X was the expensive part and the cure needed none of it. When you inherit a declined cure, re-derive what the instrument is actually being asked — the refusal may be sound while its prerequisites are an artifact of one decomposition.**

✏️ **F-2576-1 — A HANDOFF LINE-1 DESTROYED BY A *MERGE RESOLUTION* IS INVISIBLE TO `status-archive-audit`, AND IT TOOK THE OWNER'S DESK DOWN WITH IT FOR THE SECOND TIME IN THREE FIRES (measured s2576).** §4 and F-2403-1 both govern a fire that REWRITES line 1, and `status-archive-audit` is the instrument that catches a destroyed one. **A MERGE is neither: it resolves a line-1 conflict by picking one side, and the other side is discarded with no archive step anywhere in the law.** 📊 **MEASURED s2576, and the audit's blindness here is BY DESIGN and DECLARED, not a bug — `status-archive-audit.mjs:152` reads `%P` and the tool refuses any commit that does not have EXACTLY ONE parent, saying so in its own words (*"none has exactly one parent — no line-1 transition is unambiguous"*). `0cc9fe386` is a 2-parent merge whose discarded parent **IS** `bd21c0afe`, i.e. s2575's handoff — the commit whose entire subject was *"restore the OWNER'S DESK to 6"*. So the audit skips exactly the commit that did the destroying.** ⓘ **NOT a window artifact, checked rather than assumed: `bd21c0afe` sits at INDEX 3 of the 5,513 STATUS.md commits, well inside the `--limit 40` the battery uses. And the audit is not silent — it reported `LOST: 1` on this very run — but the line it names is a DIFFERENT attended line (`13:10Z`), which is what makes the miss hard to see: **the instrument is loud about one loss in the same breath that it cannot see another.** 🎯 **THE CONSEQUENCE IS THE DESK: at HEAD, s2575's handoff line-1 appears **ZERO** times in `STATUS.md` — neither as line 1 nor as an archive bullet (only its LOCK line is archived) — so the three desk items it had just restored went with it. **This is F-2575-1's class recurring by a NEW MECHANISM:** that finding diagnosed a COMPOSED desk and its cure is *"never COMPOSE the desk — carry it"*. Here nobody composed anything; a whole line was chosen at a merge and the desk rode along inside it. **A rule about how you WRITE the desk cannot reach a loss that happens at a merge.**
⚖️ **SEVERITY STATED HONESTLY AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED: realised cost ZERO and NOTHING WAS LOST — `STATUS.md` is TRACKED, git holds `bd21c0afe` forever, and s2576 recovered all three items VERBATIM from it in one command. This is NOT a false green: the desk never claimed to hold them.** 🎯 **What earns it a row is the DIRECTION and the REPETITION: the desk is the owner's decision queue, this is the SECOND silent drop of the SAME three items in three fires, and all three were verified still live before restoring — `desk-state-audit` reads `CLOSED=0 · OPEN-DESK-ONLY=6`, so not one of them had been ruled on. An item that leaves the desk without a reason is not deferred, it is FORGOTTEN.** ✅ **RECOVERED s2576: desk restored to 6, the three carried VERBATIM from `bd21c0afe`, with F-2569-2's figures re-measured rather than inherited (`421 files / ~451 MB` → **`431 files / 469.3 MB`**, still 100% attended-owned, still zero factory-side) — so the row the owner reads is current rather than three fires stale.** ➡️ **THEREFORE, BINDING, AND IT WIDENS F-2403-1 ONTO A SECOND AXIS: that clause says walk the archive chain BACKWARD until you reach a line-1 that IS archived. Walk it through **MERGE PARENTS** too — when your predecessor's line-1 is absent, ask `git log -1 --format=%P` of the commits between you and it, and read the DISCARDED side. One command, and it is the only way this route is visible.** 🚫 **NO GUARD, and the restraint is measured rather than lazy: resolving a line-1 conflict by keeping one side is LAWFUL and ROUTINE — every drain merge does it, and keeping the fresher line is usually CORRECT — so a red would fire during ordinary correct operation and be excused into uselessness inside a week (F-1460-1, the `cross-engine` fate). The cure is the recovery plus the sentence you are reading.** 💡 **REUSABLE, and it is a NEW AXIS for this streak: twenty-odd findings have asked how a MEASUREMENT can be wrong — a swallow, a guard, a cwd, a filename, a timezone, a registry, a clock, a denominator. This one asks how a *RECORD* can be wrong: it was written correctly, by a diligent fire, and then a THIRD party discarded it in an operation neither the writer nor the reader was watching. When you check that a duty was discharged, check that its OUTPUT still exists at HEAD — a commit is proof that something was written, never proof that it survived.**

✏️ **F-2575-1 — A CONTENDED BATTERY DOES NOT ONLY MOVE A TIMING *ASSERTION*, IT BLOWS A *SPAWN BOUND* — AND A BLOWN BOUND REPORTS AS A TIMEOUT, WHICH READS AS A WEDGE AND AIMS THE NEXT FIRE AT THE WRONG SUBJECT ENTIRELY.** Measured s2575 on a dry board, under an attended load spike. ✅ **THE VERDICT FIRST: NOTHING IS BROKEN. `scripts/gazette-scan-space-guard.test.mjs` run ALONE is `rc=0, 9/9 pass` in `114.9 s`, and its slowest arm — *"the scan space is DECLARED on stdout"*, which spawns the full-history `gazette-backfill-sweep` — takes `114.6 s` of its `240_000 ms` F-2430-1 bound.** 📊 **THE MEASUREMENT, one tree, the ONLY variable being what else was running: battery run 1 (load ordinary) → that leg PASSED, `125.2 s` total; battery run 2, same tree but for a baseline re-pin, machine at **load average `99.74` on 16 CPUs** → that leg reported `✖ ... (240002.849167ms)`, i.e. the bound firing to the millisecond, `280.1 s` total; the leg re-run ALONE moments later → `rc=0`, `114.9 s`. **A 2.1× swing across the bound, against a guard whose subject I never touched.** ✅ **AND THEN THE CONTROL THAT ISOLATES THE VARIABLE, which is stronger than the re-run-alone arm because it changes only the machine: once load fell to `11.10` the WHOLE battery was re-run on that same tree and the same leg passed with no timeout (`172.8 s`, every node leg green). So "run it ALONE" is NOT the explanatory variable — CONTENTION is, and the aloneness cure works only because it happens to remove it.**** ⚖️ **SEVERITY STATED HONESTLY AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED: realised cost ZERO, NOT a false green, and the bound behaved EXACTLY as F-2430-1 designed — it failed LOUD and named its subject, which is the whole point of that cure. No verdict anywhere was wrong.** 🎯 **WHAT EARNS IT A ROW IS THE DIRECTION AT THE MOMENT IT FIRES, AND IT IS A NEW AXIS ON F-2462-1. That finding established that contention manufactures findings by moving a timing ASSERTION — a `p95` against a cap — and its cure is *"run it ALONE"*. This is the same contamination arriving through the SPAWN BOUND instead, and the two do not read alike: an assertion failure prints a number against a cap and invites you to doubt the number, while a bound failure prints `ETIMEDOUT` at exactly `240002 ms` and invites you to doubt whether anything is RUNNING. **That is the wedge signature F-2429-1 and F-2563-3 describe, so the reader is pointed at `ps`, at grandchild checks, at §2.0b — a whole diagnostic ladder built for a process that is stuck, for a leg that is merely queued behind ninety-nine other runnable threads.** The tell that separates them is the ARITHMETIC, not the stack: a bound that fires at its exact value on a leg that passes alone is CONTENTION; a wedge blows every bound in the chain and ignores SIGTERM.** 📐 **AND THE HEADROOM IS THE HALF WORTH CARRYING FORWARD, because it is F-2465-2 landing on a SECOND subject: `240_000` was applied to all 83 sites MECHANICALLY by F-2430-1, and F-2465-2 already corrected it once (`fixture-teardown`, whose child is itself a battery at `326.2 s`, i.e. ABOVE the house bound). This leg is the mirror case — `114.6 s`, **48% of its bound consumed on a quiet machine** — and its child is a FULL-HISTORY scan over `marketing/outbox/` and every first-parent commit, so **its cost grows monotonically with the repo while the bound stays fixed.** The margin is not a property of the guard; it is a race between the corpus and a constant.** 🚫 **NO CURE SHIPPED, and the restraint is measured rather than lazy. Raising the bound is F-1410-2 in its named costume (*raise it until it goes green*) and would blind the one case the bound exists for; lowering it reds honest work; and pinning a duration reds on every slower machine and is excused into uselessness inside a week (F-1460-1, the `cross-engine` fate) — which is exactly why F-2413-1 and F-2414-1 BOTH declined a guard for this battery’s wall time. **The cure is this row plus the sentence in `scripts/fire.md` §3.** ➡️ **THEREFORE, BINDING, AND IT COSTS ONE COMMAND: before you report a battery red as a finding, read `uptime`. At load ≈ 100 on 16 CPUs the battery is not an instrument, and BOTH its assertions and its bounds are measuring the machine. Re-run the failing leg ALONE and report what THAT says — and say plainly in the handoff that the aggregate did not come back clean, rather than reporting a green you did not get.** 💡 **REUSABLE: F-2452-1 showed a scalar COST is a measurement with a hidden parameter (load); F-2567-1 showed a derived AGE has one (the clock). **A BOUND HAS ONE TOO, AND IT IS THE SAME PARAMETER — but where a cost is merely *quoted* wrong, a bound *converts* the hidden parameter into a categorical verdict: PASS or TIMEOUT, with no number in the failure to hint that load was the variable.** When a timeout fires at exactly its configured value, treat the configured value as the thing you measured, not the subject. (s2575, 2026-09-15)

✏️ **F-2573-1 — THE LB-01 EXPOSURE GATE’S `✅ CLEAN` VERDICT HAS BEEN STRUCTURALLY UNREACHABLE SINCE 2026-09-04, AND ITS *DISCOVERY* CHANNEL IS NOW PERMANENT NOISE ITS OWN PRESCRIBED REMEDY CANNOT CLEAR.** Measured s2573 on a dry board. ✅ **THE SAFETY VERDICT FIRST, AND IT IS INTACT: `account-class rows: 0`, verified by READING the mirror schema and every row rather than by trusting the tool — the county book now holds `kv` (36 rows, prefixes `standings:` + `assay-queue-index`) plus a `refusals` table (14 rows) whose only identity columns are a 32-hex `anon_id` and the public `profile_name`. That is the door-refusal tally s2469 shipped: county-standings class, NOT account data, so the standing encryption gate is NOT breached and every mirror committed to date was lawful.** 🔑 **THE DEFECT IS IN THE CLASSIFIER’S AXIS. A KEY/VALUE NAMESPACE ENCODES ITS CLASS *IN THE VALUE* (`session:abc`), so a prefix test reaches a verdict on every key. A RELATIONAL COLUMN DOES NOT: `refusals.profile_name` is rider-chosen free text (3 distinct today — `Claude Opus 5`, `Heat 12 Era Probe`, `sam`) and `anon_id` is a 32-hex digest (all 3 match `/^[0-9a-f]{32}$/`, verified), so NEITHER CAN EVER CARRY A PREFIX and both sit in `unrecognised` forever.** ⚙️ **AND THAT BUCKET SHORT-CIRCUITS THE VERDICT: `ledger-mirror-exposure.mjs`’s `else if (unrecognised.length)` arm sits ABOVE the `✅ CLEAN` arm, so while it is non-empty the tool CANNOT print the one sentence the LB-01 duty sends a fire to read before committing a mirror — *"every key in every mirror is county-standings class… safe to commit"*.** 📊 **MEASURED: `250` unrecognised rows, **`250` of them these two columns and `0` from `kv`**; the `refusals` table entered the series 2026-09-04 (11 of 22 mirrors carry it, 11 predate it), and because every mirror is a FULL snapshot the bucket grows by `2 x (table rows)` PER DAY — 250 today from just 14 distinct refusals. **The tool’s own remedy at the site — *"add the prefix to SAFE_PREFIXES"* — is INAPPLICABLE BY CONSTRUCTION to both columns.** ⚖️ **SEVERITY STATED HONESTLY AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED: realised cost ZERO, NOT a false green, and NOT a false red either — the battery invokes this leg WITHOUT `--strict`, so it returned 0 throughout and nothing ever reddened. The denylist arm was never impaired: the sibling guard’s `session:exposed-rider` arm proves an account identity in that very column is still caught.** 🎯 **What earns it a cure is the DIRECTION and the date: a fire following LB-01 reads a 250-line ⚠️ block it can do nothing about, every day, and a genuinely NEW class arrives as line 251 among 250 known-benign lines — the discovery channel buried exactly when the gate ARMS (F-2353-2: fixing F-MAIL-0829 routes account writes into this very DB). Under `--strict` it was rc=1 on a bucket no one can ever empty, which is F-1460-1’s decay reached by a new route: not *excused* into uselessness but UNCLEARABLE by its own remedy (F-2451-1’s "alarm with no remedy", one layer in).** 🛠️ **CURE: `SAFE_TABLE_COLUMNS` — the class of a COLUMN is a property of the SCHEMA, decided once, where the class of a key is a property of each value. `TABLE_IDENTITY_COLUMNS` already recorded the schema judgement *"harvest these"*; this records the one that had nowhere to live, *"and these are county-standings class"*. **APPLIED ONLY AFTER THE ACCOUNT DENYLIST, DELIBERATELY** — marking a column safe FIRST is the over-general cure, and the sibling guard’s existing `session:exposed-rider` arm is its reverse control. Declared on stdout ALWAYS, including the happy path (F-2208-1): a suppression nobody is shown is a suppression nobody can re-judge.** 📐 **DELTA MEASURED, NOT CLAIMED NEUTRAL (F-1274-2 governs the measurement, not the outcome — this cure is deliberately not behaviour-neutral), control asserting its own validity first (F-2215-1): default `rc 0 → 0` (the battery leg is UNAFFECTED), `--strict` `rc 1 → 0`, verdict `⚠️ → ✅`, stdout `15,168 B → 2,602 B`, `unrecognised 250 → 0`, `account 0 → 0` — and **`keys inspected 686 → 686`, the control that matters: the cure RECLASSIFIES, it does not shrink the corpus or stop looking.** 🔒 **Guarded by `scripts/ledger-mirror-column-class-guard.test.mjs` — 10 arms, 0.4 s, rooted in `test:ledger-guards`; `gate-caller-audit` PASS; the sibling 23-arm guard stays 23/23 green.** 🔬 **TEETH, 7 variants manufactured on scratch copies with the subject restored byte-identical each time and the restoration ASSERTED BY SHA: pre-cure verbatim reds 6 · over-general (column before denylist) reds the REVERSE CONTROL arm · declaration dropped reds 2 · blanket column-safe reds 4 · map emptied reds 8 · call sites reverted to the one-argument form reds 6 · harvest-every-column reds the SKIPPED-declaration arm. **All 10 arms red under at least one variant, so none is decoration.**** ⚠️ **TWO METHOD NOTES PAID FOR IN THIS FIRE. (1) MY FIRST DERIVATION HELPER REGEX-SCRAPED `SAFE_TABLE_COLUMNS` AND DIED ON `JSON.parse`: a JS object literal is not JSON (unquoted keys, single quotes). It now IMPORTS the subject’s own exported accessor — still derived, so a map edit reaches the guard in the same commit (the F-2365-1 pattern), and an underivable map REFUSES rather than passing vacuously (F-2217-1). (2) MY TEETH HARNESS DOUBLE-COUNTED EVERY RED, because `^✖ (.+?) \(` matches both the per-test line AND the failing-tests summary block; the distinct-arm count is computed from a Set and is the only figure to trust. **A reachability audit is itself an instrument, and its own miscount is indistinguishable from coverage it does not have** (F-2491-1’s note, reproduced independently).** 💡 **REUSABLE, and it is a NEW AXIS: this streak has spent thirty fires asking how a SUBJECT SET can be wrong — too narrow, wrong tree, wrong axis, a registry not a census, entries not files, no durability predicate, an undeclared denominator. **This classifier’s subject set is PERFECT — it inspects all 686 values — and its AXIS CANNOT REACH A VERDICT on part of it.** A classifier that cannot classify does not fail loudly; it files the unclassifiable into its own "go look at this" bucket, where the residue accumulates until the bucket means nothing and the clean verdict it gates becomes unreachable. **When a data source changes SHAPE — here key/value gaining a relational table — ask not whether your classifier still RUNS, but whether its axis can still reach a verdict on what it is now being handed.** (s2573, 2026-09-15)

✏️ **F-2572-1 — A RETENTION TRANSFORM MOVES THE *PATH* AS WELL AS THE BLOB, SO F-2571-1’s OWN METHOD ("search the TREES, never the names") IS NECESSARY AND NOT SUFFICIENT: A TREE SEARCH KEYED ON THE SUBJECT’s ON-DISK PATH RETURNS **EMPTY** FOR CONTENT THAT IS FULLY DURABLE.** Measured s2572 by executing the one-fire-old F-2571-1 cure across BOTH non-SAFE buckets rather than admiring it. ✅ **THE VERDICT FIRST, AND IT REPRODUCES BOTH PREDECESSORS EXACTLY: every one of the `6` non-SAFE blobs is FULLY ON ORIGIN — `UNREFERENCED` 2 (parts `5/5`, `6/6`) and `LOCAL-REF-ONLY` 4 (`2/2`, `3/3`, `2/2`, `5/5`) = **23/23 parts origin-reachable**, every holding tip on origin, `AT RISK 0`.** That is F-2571-1’s `11/11` and F-2570-1’s `12/12` re-derived independently. 🔑 **THE FINDING IS THAT MY FIRST VERIFIER SAID "NOT PRESERVED" TWICE, AND BOTH FALSE ALARMS CAME FROM ITS OWN KEYS.** (1) `git ls-tree -r <ref> -- logs/runs-archive/traces/prefetch-bounded-warming` returns **`0` paths on the very ref that holds it** — because the transform **RELOCATED** the content to `artifacts/prefetch-bounded-warming/`. Measured: that ref holds `84` paths, `78` of which mention the slug, and **`0`** under the subject’s on-disk directory. (2) The manifest’s `parts[]` entries carry `path` + `bytes` + `sha256` and **NO `gitBlob`**, so a verifier keyed on `p.gitBlob` scores every part unreachable and prints `parts 0/5`. ⚖️ **SEVERITY STATED HONESTLY AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED: realised cost ZERO, nothing is at risk, nothing was lost, no verdict was published wrong, and this is NOT a false green — it is the same FALSE URGENCY class as F-2566-1, F-2570-1 and F-2571-1.** 🎯 **What earns it a rung is WHERE it sits: those three cured the false urgency in the BUCKET LABEL; this one is in the VERIFIER BUILT TO CHECK THE LABEL. A fire acting on either false reading files a `389.4 MB` Mistake #11 durability hole that does not exist, or pushes bytes already on origin — the exact acts F-2571-1 forbids, reached by obeying F-2571-1.** ➡️ **THEREFORE, BINDING: the only sound key is the manifest’s own `gitBlob` field, plus resolving each part **BY PATH IN THAT REF’s TREE**. Not the ref name, not the subject’s directory, and not a `gitBlob` on the parts — `BACKUP-MANIFEST.json` records `gitBlob`/`bytes`/`sha256` precisely so the question is decidable when every other coordinate has moved.** ⓘ **A corroborating name datum, since it is the same lesson twice: the holder is `refs/heads/archive/prefetch-bounded-warming-s2519` LOCALLY and `refs/remotes/origin/save/prefetch-bounded-warming-s2519` on origin — the salvage lifecycle renamed `save/` → `archive/` on one side only, so even the ref’s own name differs between the two places you might look for it.** 🔎 **AND WHAT CAUGHT IT IS WORTH MORE THAN THE CURE, BECAUSE IT INVERTS MISTAKE #4’s USUAL POLARITY: the INHERITED NUMBER did. s2571 banked `11 of 11` and my instrument said `0/5`; the disagreement is the only reason I re-read my own extractor instead of publishing a 389 MB alarm.** Normally an inherited claim is the thing to distrust — here it was the CONTROL that exposed my own defective instrument. ➡️ **So BANK YOUR PARTS COUNT beside the verdict: it is the one figure a successor can use to catch a broken verifier, and it costs a line.** 🚫 **NO GUARD, and the restraint is INHERITED rather than re-argued: an untracked file in a working tree is a LAWFUL routine state, so a red would fire during ordinary correct operation and be excused into uselessness inside a week (F-1460-1, the `cross-engine` fate) — the same restraint F-2357-1, F-2389-1, F-2484-1, F-2485-1, F-2487-1, F-2488-1, F-2489-1, F-2495-1, F-2569-1, F-2570-1 and F-2571-1 all took for this very sweep. The cure is the sentence you are reading.** 💡 **REUSABLE, and it is a NEW AXIS after *how WIDE is the selector* (F-2358-1), *can its axis EXPRESS the defect* (F-2389-1), *which TREE* (F-2484-1), *a registry is not a census* (F-2485-1), *how many subjects did the author VISIT* (F-2487-1/F-2488-1), *entries vs files* (F-2490-1), *no durability predicate* (F-2560-1) and *an undeclared denominator* (F-2561-1): all of those interrogate the SUBJECT SET or the PREDICATE. **This one interrogates the INSTRUMENT THAT CHECKS THE PREDICATE.** When a cure says content may be preserved "in another SHAPE", remember SHAPE includes the PATH — a transform that splits, relocates and re-names is free to move every coordinate except the one the manifest records on purpose.

✏️ **F-2571-1 — `UNREFERENCED` OVER-REPORTS BY THE *SAME* RETENTION TRANSFORM F-2570-1 FOUND ONE FIRE EARLIER, AND THAT CURE’S SENTENCE NAMES ONLY ITS SIBLING BUCKET.** F-2570-1 bound the reader to ask, *before acting on a LOCAL-REF-ONLY verdict*, whether a retention transform already preserved the content in another shape. The four-bucket predicate has TWO non-SAFE buckets a transform can falsify, printed four lines apart in the same sweep output, and only one was named. **MEASURED s2571, controls asserted first (F-2215-1): `35,060` tracked · `hashed 97 of 97` · `121,653` remote objects → `AT RISK 0 · UNREFERENCED 2 / 389.4 MB · LOCAL-REF-ONLY 4 / 390.1 MB · SAFE 91`. Both UNREFERENCED files — the `artifacts/deploy-budget-production-probe/{e1-probe,e1-reviewed}/**/trace.zip` pair — are FULLY ON ORIGIN as ordered 40 MiB parts on `origin/save/deploy-probe-evidence-s2520`; VERIFIED not inferred: both `gitBlob` ids in that ref’s `BACKUP-MANIFEST.json` with bytes + sha256 (`177,232,908`/`18012f9b…`, `212,141,445`/`5cd4c743…`), `11 of 11` parts origin-reachable.** 🔑 **METHOD: a ref-NAME grep returns FIVE refs and none is the right one (`save/deploy-budget-hard-verdict-s2518` reads like the match and holds ZERO paths under the dir); asking every ref’s TREE returns exactly the one. A retention ref is named for the FIRE THAT MINTED IT, never for what it holds.** ⚖️ **SEVERITY HONEST AND NOT INFLATED: realised cost ZERO, nothing lost, nothing losable, NOT a false green — the same FALSE URGENCY class as F-2566-1 and F-2570-1, on a third bucket.** 🎯 **DIRECTION: after F-2566-1 correctly killed the `git gc` fuse, the reader is left holding *"the reachable fuses are the DISK and `git clean -fd`"* — i.e. a 389.4 MB Mistake #11 durability hole. It is not a hole. Acting on it means either pushing 389 MB already on origin or filing it to the desk beside F-2563-2’s ~20 GB version of the same question.** ➡️ **CURE (landed, `scripts/fire.md` §2E beside F-2570-1): apply the retention-transform test to EVERY non-SAFE bucket — AT RISK, UNREFERENCED and LOCAL-REF-ONLY alike.** ⓘ **The AT RISK arm is UNEXERCISED (0 today) and labelled as such rather than claimed (F-2182-1).** 🚫 **NO GUARD — restraint INHERITED from the ten prior findings in this chain (F-1460-1).** 💡 **REUSABLE: a cure written against a MULTI-BUCKET predicate selects on the BUCKET. F-2566-1 corrected one bucket’s label, F-2570-1 another’s, four lines apart in one paragraph by the same reasoning, and neither looked at the other’s — F-2357-1’s orthogonal-axes lesson with the axes one ROW apart instead of one TOOL apart.** (s2571, 2026-09-15)

✏️ **F-2570-1 — `LOCAL-REF-ONLY` OVER-REPORTS FOR A REASON THAT HAS NOTHING TO DO WITH DURABILITY: A DELIBERATE RETENTION TRANSFORM PRESERVES *CONTENT* WHILE DESTROYING *BLOB IDENTITY*, SO F-2487-1’s "EXACT" TEST RETURNS **NO** FOR BYTES THAT ARE FULLY OFFSITE.** F-2487-1 mechanised F-2483-1’s fifth test (*is this content already committed in another form?*) as blob identity against `refs/remotes/` and called it **EXACT where prose equivalence is not**. It is exact about BLOBS, which is not the same claim. 📊 MEASURED s2570 on main’s evidence-prefixed untracked set, each read’s CONTROL asserted first (F-2215-1 — `35,060` tracked files, `hashed 97 of 97`, `121,626` remote-reachable objects, so a zero is an ANSWER): **`AT RISK 0 · UNREFERENCED 2 · LOCAL-REF-ONLY 4 / 372.0 MB · SAFE 91`**. All four LOCAL-REF-ONLY are `logs/runs-archive/traces/prefetch-bounded-warming/*/trace.zip`, and every one is **FULLY ON ORIGIN** — split into ordered 40 MiB parts on `origin/save/prefetch-bounded-warming-s2519` (`e406a8d5c`, s2519’s F-2518-3 retention), with `gitBlob` + `bytes` + `sha256` per file in that ref’s `BACKUP-MANIFEST.json` and a documented `cat …/trace.zip.parts/part-* > trace.zip` reconstruction. ✅ **VERIFIED, NOT INFERRED: all four `gitBlob` ids present in the manifest, and 12 of 12 parts origin-reachable.** 🔑 **THE CORROBORATION IS EXACT: the SAME directory holds a FIFTH trace from the SAME run and the SAME retention commit, and it scores SAFE. The discriminator is not durability — it is `> 40 MiB`. 4 of 4 over the split threshold read LOCAL-REF-ONLY; the 1 under it (35.2 MB) reads SAFE. The bucket is reporting A FILE SIZE.** ⚖️ SEVERITY HONEST AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED: realised cost ZERO, nothing at risk, nothing lost, NOT a false green — `AT RISK` is genuinely 0 and every reading this streak published was TRUE. It is a FALSE URGENCY, F-2566-1’s shape on a new axis. 🎯 DIRECTION is what earns it: the label reads *"dies with this disk"* and the remedy is *"push them