as wrong, and this is NOT a false green — it is the opposite, a FALSE URGENCY, an alarm louder than the truth.** 🎯 **What earns it a row is the DIRECTION, and it fails BOTH ways around one date five days out. BEFORE it: a fire reading the named fuse spends its budget beating a deadline that does not exist — by salvaging to a `save/*` ref, or worse by pushing `371 MB` to origin, which F-2563-1/F-2563-2 rule OWNER-CALL territory and which F-2565-1 forbids in so many words. AFTER it: the reader concludes the bytes are GONE and stops looking — a FALSE LOSS REPORT about files sitting on disk, i.e. the RETENTION LAW’s own subject (Mistake #11) reported backwards.** ⓘ **THE HONEST FULL PICTURE, because naming the wrong fuse IS the defect: these two do have fuses — the DISK, and an untracked-file sweep (`git clean -fd`, which `lane-runner-v3.sh:550` runs on a lane refresh and which no fire runs on main). The one fuse the label named is the one that cannot fire. A secondary observation NOT leaned on: both objects are PACKED rather than loose, so a `gc` must explode them before pruning them by mtime — but the argument needs only that the file is on disk.** 🛠️ **CURE: the discriminator now stands at the site in `scripts/fire.md` §2E, beside the bucket definition itself — which is F-2436-1’s duty (*"when you retire a number, grep the LAW SURFACES for it in the same commit as the measurement"*) applied to a SENTENCE rather than a number — and F-2560-2’s deadline is RESTATED, NOT DELETED, at its own site. No instrument changed and no verdict moved; the judgement on the two files is unchanged and still correct: DO NOT SALVAGE, DO NOT PUSH, DO NOT DELETE.** 🚫 **NO GUARD, and the restraint is MEASURED rather than lazy: the mechanical key would be *"a dated deadline whose date has passed"*, and ~99.9% of this file’s `2,546` date tokens are HISTORICAL RECORDS (*"measured s2565"*, *"owner 2026-08-26"*) — so it would fire on ordinary correct prose and be excused into uselessness inside a week (F-1460-1, the `cross-engine` fate). Same restraint F-2450-1 took for a live instruction and F-2492-1 for an expired convention.** 💡 **REUSABLE, and it is a NEW AXIS on this factory’s most-repeated finding (F-2153-1 · F-2204-1 · F-2350-1 · F-2360-1 · F-2365-1 · F-2403-1 · F-2414-1 · F-2436-1 · F-2449-1 · F-2451-1): in every one of those the cure was written where its reader never looks and the author did not know which surface governed. **HERE THE AUTHOR WAS IN THE RIGHT FILE AND STOPPED ONE STEP SHORT.** A finding’s *reusable half* is written as a lesson for the future, so it reads as complete the moment it is written — but when that lesson CONTRADICTS AN EARLIER CLAIM, it is not a lesson, it is an EDIT INSTRUCTION. ➡️ **Grep for the claim you just refuted — in the ledger AND in the law — and retire it in the same commit as the measurement. A refutation that is only a lesson leaves the refuted sentence load-bearing, and the next reader meets the sentence, not the lesson.** Law: `scripts/fire.md` §2E (appended to the F-2560-2 four-bucket clause). Related: [F-2565-1], [F-2560-2], [F-2560-1], [F-2563-1], [F-2436-1], [F-2450-1].

