o** `HeadlessContractSim` for it. The two `mode: escort` constructions in that spec belong to `e3-canyon-works`. **The master therefore demanded a headless proof while its own NO #3 forbade the only construction path an unadmitted contract has.** ✅ **THE RUNNER WAS RIGHT, AND THE MASTER PRE-AUTHORIZED THIS OUTCOME IN THE SAME SENTENCE:** *"If it turns out no such path can drive your assertions without admitting the contract, STOP and report it as a finding — that is a genuine design fork about what admission means, and it belongs to the owner, not to this run."* That is a well-written stop condition doing its job; **the cost of the defect was one run, not a corrupted merge**, and that is the whole value of writing stop conditions into masters. ⭐ **WHAT s1474 GOT RIGHT, RECORDED SO THE NEXT AUTHOR DOES NOT OVER-CORRECT:** its browser-chain trace verified sound (`FerrisWheel.damage()` drops `spinning` on first hit → `syncFerrisWheelPower()` mirrors it via `set-node-online` from the sim tick → the 24 W producer starves both 6 W pavilion lamps → coverage drops → `autoSecureWaveForRun()` goes `MAX_SAFE_INTEGER`), as did its measurements that `twist.powerGrid` and `twist.dayNightCycle` are **already** socketed and that fairground has **no baron**. The socket SCOPE — extracting a headlessly-constructible Fair Wheel core out of `src/entities/FerrisWheel.ts` — remains sound and re-usable the moment the proof path is ruled on. 💡 **THE REUSABLE CLASS, and it is a new one: the master checked exhaustively that the SUBJECT was ready and never checked that the INSTRUMENT could be CONSTRUCTED.** Every probe it wrote asked about the wheel, the twist keys, the tick budget, the baron, the safe-dupe trap — all about the contents of the harness. **None asked whether the harness door opens for this contract.** ➡️ **Authoring rule earned here: an era-socket master must prove the harness can be INSTANTIATED for its target before it specifies one line of what to do inside it** — one construction probe, or one grep of `SUPPORTED_CONTRACTS` for the contract id, would have caught this before 124,606 tokens were spent. 🔺 **THE OWNER QUESTION — one word, and it unblocks the last E3 era-socket:** is there a **diagnostics-only** construction path that is explicitly **NOT** an admission? **RECOMMENDED (c):** a boot flag (e.g. `diagnostic: true`) that bypasses the support gate for MEASUREMENT while leaving `SUPPORTED_CONTRACTS` — the playability CLAIM — untouched, so the census headline stays **AGENT-READY 3 of 4** and the contract stays rejected until the crowd half is ruled on. **REJECTED BY THIS FIRE, with reasons: (a)** admitting `e3-fairground`, because admission asserts an agent can PLAY a contract whose advertised crowd-flock escort objective still has no consumer anywhere (`twist.fairground.crowdFlocks` sits in `DECLARED_INERT_PATHS` deliberately); **(b)** authoring a `modes` entry into contract data, which is inventing vocabulary to fit a harness — **Mistake #14, reject-do-not-stretch.** ⛔ **DO NOT RE-QUEUE THE MASTER UNCHANGED** — an identical retry of a premise already proven false is forbidden (§7.5); leaf `e3-fairground-socket` is now `blocked` / `blockClass: owner-fork` and `drain-block-check` STOPs on it.

- ✅ **F-1475-2 (s1475, AUTHORED — SHIPPED s1476 at `1c8b344a`) — CLOSING F-1473-1: PIN THE POST-DEATH CRAWLER 3D STATE ON THE LITE AND FAILED TIERS.** `tasks/lane-f1473-1-crawler-dispose-tier-pin.md` → lane-a (leaf `f1473-1-crawler-dispose-tier-pin`), **TEST-ONLY**, firewall is the single file `e2e/wire-crawler-3d.spec.ts`. It closes the GATE F-1473-1 wrote for itself, verbatim: *"closes when a spec kills the full component ladder on the lite tier and pins the intended post-death `crawler3dState`."* 🔑 **GROUND TRUTH RE-DERIVED FROM THE CODE, NOT INHERITED FROM THE FINDING:** the broadened guard is live in `src/systems/CrawlerBossSystem.ts` as the single line `if (components.size === 0 && this.crawler3dState !== 'disposed') this.disposeCrawler3d('disposed');`, and `private disposeCrawler3d(nextState)` assigns `this.crawler3dState = nextState` before publishing to the canvas dataset — **so the expected post-death value is `disposed` on every tier.** The master states that expectation up front and rules that a differing measurement is a **FINDING**, not a licence to edit the assertion to match: *a test that pins whatever happened blesses the bug it was written to catch.* ⚠️ **THE F-1475-1 LESSON WAS APPLIED IN THE SAME FIRE THAT LEARNED IT — I PROBED WHETHER THE HARNESS COULD BE CONSTRUCTED BEFORE WRITING ONE LINE OF SCOPE, AND IT COULD NOT, AS-IS.** The existing kill ladder helper `destroyComponent()` calls `awaitMounted()`, which asserts `data-crawler3d-state` is `ready` on a 15 s timeout — but the lite tier publishes `lite` and the invalid-GLB tier publishes `failed`, **so a naive reuse fails 15 s per component on an assertion unrelated to the subject.** Everything else in that helper is tier-agnostic (suspend/restore HP edit, `crawlerParts`, `setBalance`, `launchBlastAt`, the gone-poll), so the mount-wait is a clean seam and scope item 1 is exactly that. **The master gives the trap away deliberately rather than letting the runner buy it** — which is the difference 