ty `artifacts/**` as a pre-flight failure; or compare them perceptually rather than by byte. **All three are policy choices with a cost, so this is a RULING, not a mechanical fix.** **GATE: closes when a ruling picks one of the three for tracked screenshot evidence AND the pre-flight clean-vs-main test is reconciled with it.** ⓘ Scope note, stated so nobody over-reads this: measured for `wire-crawler-3d` + `wire-railcar-3d`, `desktop-chrome`, this machine. Other specs that write screenshots are **unmeasured** and are likely the same class.

- ✅ **F-1476-1 (s1476, drain of `f1473-1-crawler-dispose-tier-pin`) — THE RETAINED RENDERER-COUNT "BASELINES" ARE NOT A REPRODUCIBLE PROPERTY OF A COMMIT. THEY ARE PARTLY RUN-TO-RUN NOISE, AND THE SPEC REWRITES 12 TRACKED FILES EVERY RUN.** NON-BLOCKING for the merged slice, proved by a control arm. ⚠️ **THIS ROW WAS REWRITTEN LATER IN THE SAME FIRE THAT FILED IT — the first version asserted the baselines were "stale on main" and prescribed refreshing them "in a commit that names the main-side change responsible". I then MEASURED that instruction and it is partly unsatisfiable: for the `calls`/`triangles` component there IS no responsible change to name.** 🔬 **HOW IT WAS MEASURED, since the first framing looked airtight:** running `e2e/wire-crawler-3d.spec.ts` dirties 10 PNGs plus both `artifacts/wire-crawler-3d/renderer-counts-*.json`, with deltas across all five recorded phases (desktop `coldBaseline` `calls 72→74`, `triangles 146026→147708`, `geometries 80→81`). A three-way comparison first cleared the slice: control on **pure main** vs committed = DIFFERENT; **merged arm vs control arm = BYTE-IDENTICAL**; so the slice causes zero renderer change and the drift belongs to main. **Then the attempt to attribute that drift is where the real finding lives.** A bisect harness was built and PROVED CONSTRUCTIBLE first (the F-1475-1 lesson applied): a detached worktree with **the instrument PINNED and the subject VARIED** — today's `e2e/wire-crawler-3d.spec.ts` + `playwright.config.ts` + `scripts/external-server-guard.mjs` checked out over each candidate commit, **14.4 s per step**. ⓘ *The overlay needs that third file because today's config requires the F-1457-1 guard that only landed 08-05; without it every old commit dies `MODULE_NOT_FOUND` — a harness that would have failed identically at all 1,092 commits and converged on an innocent one.* 📉 **The predicate then failed its own validation, BOTH ways:** committed `72` (07-25) → `73` (07-31) → **`72`** (08-02) → `74` (main) is **non-monotonic**, so bisect's precondition is violated outright. The discriminating control — **the same commit `a1ec923b8`, same shell, back-to-back** — returned `calls 72 / triangles 146026` and then `calls 73 / triangles 146028`. ➡️ **The ±1 `calls` / ±2 `triangles` component is NOISE, not drift, and no commit can be blamed for it.** ⚖️ **What survives as possibly real:** `geometries 80→81` held steady at 80 across both runs at `a1ec923b8` and reads 81 on main, as does the large `+1682` triangle step — those are **unattributed and NOT bisectable by this predicate**; attributing them needs a denoised statistic (n runs per commit, compare distributions) rather than a byte comparison. 💡 **THE REUSABLE LESSON, which is bigger than this artifact: a number committed as "retained evidence" implies a reproducibility it was never measured to have.** Every reader since 07-25 — including this fire's own first draft, and s1475's standing order to confirm the baselines "byte-unchanged" — treated byte-equality as the pass condition for a quantity that is not byte-stable. A fire obeying that order literally would have blocked a clean slice on someone else's noise. **GATE: closes when either (a) the counts are re-characterised with a measured noise band and the artifact records a tolerance instead of a byte, or (b) the spec stops writing them into the tracked tree.** Until then the churn is the **F-1407-1 artifact-churn class** — any lane or fire running this spec is left with 12 dirty tracked files, which can trip a pre-flight's clean-vs-main test. **Do NOT "refresh" these baselines as a drive-by: it would bless one sample of a noisy quantity as truth and restart the same misreading.** 📐 **MEASURED s1477 — THE DENOISED STATISTIC THE GATE ASKED FOR NOW EXISTS, AND IT CORRECTS THIS ROW ON ONE POINT OF RECORD.** `artifacts/s1477-noise/REPORT.md` (`1b64d48b1`): **crawler n=12, railcar n=10, one commit `659f4efe0`, instrument pinned, `--workers=1`, same shell** — subject fixed, so any spread IS the band. **(1)** Most metrics are already byte-stable: **18 of 20** (crawler), **10 of 18** (railcar). **(2)** `geometries`/`textures` jitter is **COMMON-MODE — every phase-to-phase delta is EXACT** (all 6 railcar phase pairs, single value across all 10 runs: `geometries` 8, 5, 5, −3, −3, 0). The absolutes move; the differences do not, which is why the railcar spec’s live `geometries - baseline.geometries < 8` assertion is sound and has never flaked. **(3)** `calls`/`triangles` jitter **independently per phase**, with a `+1 call ⟺ +2 triangles` signature in **every** sample of **both** specs — the fingerprint of a single 2-triangle quad present or absent in the sampled frame, i.e. a sampling race rather than a rendering change. ⚠️ **THE CORRECTION: this row recorded `geometries 80→81` as "stable across both runs… unattributed and NOT bisectable". At n=12 `coldBaseline.geometries` is HARD-STABLE at 81 (12/12), so it was never the noisy component — it is a reproducible property of the commit and IS attributable.** ⚠️ **AND THE TRAP THE MEASUREMENT EXPOSED: a metric cannot be classified by its NAME.** The same `geometries` is hard-stable in the crawler’s `coldBaseline` and noisy in **four** railcar phases — noise is a property of the **(spec, phase, metric) triple, measured**, so any global whitelist is wrong on this evidence. ✓ **VERIFIED s1477 and load-bearing for the choice of cure: NOTHING READS THESE FILES** — `git grep renderer-count` over all tracked files returns the two specs that WRITE them and otherwise only prose (reviews, BACKLOG, STATUS, masters). No consumer, no guard, no assertion against the committed JSON. ➡️ **CURE QUEUED s1477 as gate disjunct (a), not (b): `tasks/lane-f1476-1-renderer-count-tolerance.md` → lane-a**, goal leaf `f1476-1-renderer-count-tolerance`. **(a) beats (b) because the deltas are exact and carry the regression signal, so "stop writing them" would discard real evidence to fix churn** — the task records exact values + exact deltas, bands with provenance for the jittery triples, and makes an out-of-band value FAIL loudly, converting a write-only churn source into an actual guard. **`mobile-chrome` remains UNSAMPLED — the master orders the runner to measure it (n≥8) rather than copy the desktop bands.** The closing prohibition below still stands and is restated in the master: do NOT refresh the old-format baselines as a drive-by. ✅ **CURED AND MERGED s1478 at `3ab612ed647f8e2fe7a697faa2213ba9cdbf433e`** (review `reviews/f1476-1-renderer-count-tolerance.md`, lane tip `7b934b643`, base `14028bd8e`). Gate disjunct **(a) tolerance-not-byte** shipped: `e2e/renderer-count-artifact.ts` turns the four artifacts from write-only evidence into **live expectations** — exact values where s1477 measured hard stability (`coldBaseline.geometries: 81`, 12/12), `{measured, tolerance}` bands with one-quantum headroom where it jitters, and **exact** phase-to-phase `geometries`/`textures` deltas where the jitter is common-mode. Every band carries a `provenance` block (`n`, `commit`, `project`, `headroom`) so it is re-derivable, and the runner measured `mobile-chrome` itself rather than copying desktop bands (`artifacts/f1476-1/samples-*-mobile-chrome.json`). 📊 **Gates, all re-run on the merged tree rather than inherited:** tsc clean · build ✓ 2.28s · slice specs **14 passed** both projects at `--workers=1` · `test:node-guards` **rc=0** (run because a new `e2e/` module is a collection surface) · zero console errors (asserted in-spec). ✅ **BOTH ACCEPTANCE TESTS REPRODUCED, NOT TAKEN ON TRUST — and the second is the load-bearing one:** (1) after a full gate run `git status -- artifacts/` shows **0** `renderer-counts-*.json` entries, down from all four every run; (2) a **manufactured** out-of-band band `[0, 1]` made the spec fail with the band branch’s own message — `renderer count coldBaseline.calls=74 outside band [0, 1]`, **1 failed** — then restored byte-identical. A passing guard never executes its violation path, so its green says nothing about whether it can fail (s1299/s1300 standard). ⓘ Merge was **zero-conflict**: all 12 lane paths LANE-TOUCHED only against the 16 main moved since base. **No `src/` bytes**, so the F-1460-1 sim-pin trigger does not fire, DEPLOY correctly skips, and **no GZ-01 gazette item is owed** — the filter law wants a player-visible change, and this one is invisible in play.
