- ✅ **F-1471-3 (s1471 — CURED AND PROVEN FAIL-CLOSED s1472 at `0f9a798b`; GATE CLOSED exactly as it was written) — `desk-declaration-guard` READ A THREE-DAY-STALE DESK AND PASSED, BECAUSE IT MATCHES `OWNER DESK` BUT FIRES WRITE `OWNER'S DESK`.** `scripts/desk-declaration-guard.mjs:93` takes the **first** STATUS.md line containing the literal `OWNER DESK`. Fires have been writing the possessive form **`OWNER'S DESK`**, which does not contain that substring — so line-1 never matched and `.find()` fell through to the newest **archived** handoff bullet that happened to use the bare form: **line 106, s1414`s desk of 2026-08-03.** ⚠️ **THE FAILURE MODE IS FAIL-OPEN, WHICH IS WHY NOBODY SAW IT: archived desks are by construction already declared** (their items got rows days ago), so the guard reported **`desk F-IDs: 145 · undeclared: 0 · PASS`** — a confident green about a desk three days dead. It was caught only because s1471`s handoff happened to write the bare form, at which point the live desk resolved (**7 F-IDs**) and **4 undeclared items surfaced instantly** (F-1461-1/4/5/6, all four buried inside OTHER findings` rows and three of them inside rows marked ✅ SHIPPED — the F-1328-3 shape the guard exists to catch). 💡 **The guard`s own header (`:55`) already states the principle it violates:** *"a guard that cannot find the desk list, or finds zero F-IDs in it, must NOT pass"*. It has a REFUSING path for **zero** F-IDs but none for **finding the wrong line** — and a stale line is indistinguishable from a fresh one by count alone. **The cure is NOT to standardise the prose** (that re-hides it behind a convention no mechanism enforces): match both forms, and **refuse unless the matched line is STATUS.md line-1**. **GATE: CLOSED s1472 — the fixture the gate names is now a test.** `scripts/desk-declaration-guard.test.mjs` holds *"MANUFACTURED F-1471-3: an archived desk must not MASK an undeclared LIVE item"*: line-1 carries a real desk in the POSSESSIVE spelling with an undeclared item, an archive bullet below carries a clean desk in the bare spelling, and the guard **exits 1 naming the live id**. Its sibling proves the other half — a headerless handoff **refuses (exit 2)** instead of reading the archive. 🔧 **THE CURE, both defects:** `deskIds()` reads **STATUS.md line 1 only**, matches **all four spellings actually used** (measured: bare 243 · `OWNER'S` 484 · curly `OWNER’S` 15 · `OWNERS` 9), and takes the **LAST** occurrence on the line so an upstream prose mention loses to the real tail header. 📐 **PROVEN ON THE REAL BOARD, not only on fixtures — the arm that matters:** reconstructing STATUS.md as it stood at s1471 and rewriting *only* the desk word to the majority spelling, the **OLD parser reads 145 F-IDs (the s1414 archive) and the NEW reads the live 8**; control arm (s1471's verbatim bare spelling, the one case the old parser handled) both agree at 8. ⚠️ **A SEPARATOR WHITELIST WAS THE OBVIOUS DESIGN AND WAS REFUTED BY MEASUREMENT BEFORE IT WAS BUILT:** requiring `:` or a dash after the desk word looks safe, but across all **617** historical line-1s carrying a desk word, `" — "` leads at 237 while `":**"` is 43, `".**"` is 40 and **253 are bare parentheticals**, with a long tail of `" to"`/`" is"`/`" as"` — that rule would have failed **CLOSED** on hundreds of legitimate headers. Swapping a fail-open for a fail-closed is not a cure. 🧹 **CLASS FIX, not instance:** the guard's OWN test file re-implemented the same broken selector by hand (`:178`) to check the grandfather list, so the test read the same stale archive as its subject; it imports `deskIds` now. ✅ **Mid-fire the guard SKIPs on an `ACTIVE` lock line** — `test:node-guards` runs inside every drain battery, when line-1 is the fire's own lock and no desk exists yet; the gate bites in `test:ledger-guards`, the last act, after the handoff commit. **EVIDENCE: `test:node-guards` 299 tests / 296 pass / 0 fail / 3 skipped, rc=0** (292 before; **7 new tests, each manufacturing a violation and driving a specific exit code**). See also **F-1472-1**, the larger finding this cure uncovered.
- 🟡 **F-1472-1 (s1472, MEASURED — ENFORCED THIS FIRE UNDER AN OPEN VETO WINDOW) — THE OWNER'S-DESK HEADER IS A LAPSED CONVENTION: OF 1249 LINE-1s IN `STATUS.md`, 617 CARRY A DESK WORD AND 632 DO NOT — AND AMONG THE LAST ~55 FIRES ONLY ABOUT 5 WROTE ONE.** Curing F-1471-3 meant reading every line-1 the factory has ever written, and the parser bug turned out to be the *smaller* half of what that measurement found. The other 632 route owner items as bare **`🔺 **F-xxxx-x OPEN**`** runs inside a `NEXT: (A)/(B)/(C)` paragraph — sampled directly at s1469, s1468, s1466, s1465, s1464, s1463, s1461, s1450, none of which carry any desk header at all — **so for most of this guard's life there was no live desk on line-1 for it to find.** 🎯 **THE TWO DEFECTS HID EACH OTHER, WHICH IS THE REUSABLE PART:** the parser fell through to an archived desk *because* line-1 usually had none, and the fall-through always looked clean *because* archived desks are by construction already declared. Neither could surface alone — a guard reading the wrong line reports a green board, and a lapsed convention leaves nothing to compare it against. That is why **137 fires** passed over it. ⚖️ **RULED AND MECHANISED THIS FIRE RATHER THAN PARKED, under §7.4 (owner absent + reversible + no new scope):** `test:desk-declaration` now **REFUSES (exit 2)** on a handoff line-1 with no desk header, and `scripts/fire.md` §4 states the duty so the next fire is not ambushed by a red it did not cause. The reasoning: routing items to Robin as loose prose is the *same* invisibility the Completeness Law forbids, one level up from the undeclared-row case this guard already gates — and a guard that cannot find its subject must never report PASS (the F-1251-2 principle **already written in this guard's own header at `:54`**, which had no reachable refusal path for "found the WRONG line"). Cost to comply: one phrase per handoff. 🔻 **VETO WINDOW — Robin: one word reverses the enforcement** (drop the `kind:'none'` refusal to