- 🔎 **F-2260-1 `desk-birth-guard` PICKED ITS WINDOW BY "THE SECOND MATCHING COMMIT", WHICH IS THIS FIRE'S OWN ADDENDUM — SO THE WINDOW COLLAPSED ONTO ITSELF (s2260, NON-BLOCKING, CURED SAME FIRE).** F-2259-1 cured the parser that decides which ROWS enter the PASS. This is the selector one level up: which COMMITS those rows are drawn from at all. `previousHandoffCommit()` took `handoffs[1]` from the list of `STATUS.md` commits whose subject matches `/^s\d+ handoff/` — but a fire that re-runs the battery after a correction emits a SECOND matching commit (`sNNNN handoff addendum:` / `amendment:` / `correction:`), so `handoffs[1]` is **the same fire's own earlier handoff** and the window shrinks to the minutes between them. The BACKLOG diff is then empty, `owner-gated rows filed` reads 0, and the guard prints PASS — accurately, about a set it built too small. 📊 **MEASURED over all 2,226 matching commits: 83 of 2,136 sessions emit more than one, and replayed at each such fire's FINAL matching commit the window start lands on the SAME fire 83 times out of 83 — never once on the previous fire.** It is not a historical curiosity: **8 of the last 30 fires did it** (s2252, s2251 ×3, s2247 ×3, s2236, s2235, s2233, s2232, s2231). ⚖️ **SEVERITY, HONESTLY AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED: LATENT — and I looked for the harm rather than asserting its absence.** Replaying BOTH windows across all 83: **59 are comparable on both arms** (the other 24 had a headerless line-1 at that commit, the lapsed-desk-convention population F-1472-1 measured, so neither arm reaches a verdict); of those 59 the correct window sees **MORE owner-gated rows in 3** and produces **ZERO new FAIL verdicts** — every row a collapsed window missed had already been desked by hand. **So this has never cost a desk entry. What it cost is the guarantee that the PASS means what it says.** 🔑 **WHY NO CENSUS IN THIS STREAK COULD SEE IT: every discriminator built since s2204 asks how a corpus comes back EMPTY, ABSENT, STALE or UNREADABLE. This corpus is none of those — `git log` succeeded, the filter matched, the list was long, and the selector simply indexed into the wrong element.** The subject regex is sound and was not the defect; `/^s\d+ handoff/` has **ZERO false positives** across the newest 400 matched commits (every one really does write a `lock CLEARED` line-1), and the 161 unmatched `handoff`-mentioning subjects are all lock lines and post-handoff corrections, correctly excluded. **The bug was an OFF-BY-IDENTITY, not an off-by-parse.** 🚦 **THE CURE IS AN IDENTITY TEST, NOT A WIDENING — and that distinction is exactly what F-2259-1 was decided on one fire earlier.** There, teaching the parser more row shapes admitted 168 rows and produced three verdicts, all false, so the denominator was DECLARED and membership left alone. Here the selector is naming the wrong commit, so skipping same-session entries **restores the window the docstring already claimed** without admitting anything new — and the replay proves it manufactures **zero** new reds. The returned `session` is the always-on declaration (F-2208-1): `window start : <sha> (s2259 handoff)` lets a reader see a collapse on sight, which a bare sha never could; the skip count is printed only when non-zero, because an advisory that fires when there is nothing to advise decays into a formality. ✅ **BEHAVIOUR-NEUTRAL BEFORE LANDING (F-1274-2): both selectors replayed as-of every one of the 2,226 matching commits — IDENTICAL in 2,136 (96.0%), differing only at the 90 positions inside the 83 duplicate sessions, which IS the cure.** Legacy suite `desk-birth-row-denominator-guard.test.mjs` 10/10 green. Per s2259's lesson the pre-cure control was materialised with its **whole transitive relative-import closure** (`corpus-tree.mjs`, `desk-declaration-guard.mjs`) and asserted its own validity — both arms exporting the subject — before any difference was believed. 🔒 `scripts/desk-birth-window-start-guard.test.mjs`, **8 arms**, rooted in `test:ledger-guards`; the fixture files an owner fork in the window and then emits N same-session addenda, so the collapse is manufactured rather than argued. **Each over-general cure is caught by exactly the reverse control built for it:** PRE-CURE verbatim reds 1+2+3+4+7+8 · refuse-on-ANY-skip reds 1+2+4+5+7 (**arm 5 is its control**) · PARTIAL, declare-but-do-not-skip, reds 1+2+4+7+8 · off-by-one reds 4+7+8 · declare-only-on-failure reds 2+3 · unconditional advisory reds **6 ALONE**. ⚠️ **THE PRE-CURE RED IS PRICED, NOT HEADLINED: only THREE of its six are SUBSTANTIVE detections** (arms 1 and 4, a collapsed window PASSING over an undesked owner fork, and arm 8, proceeding where it should refuse) — arms 2, 3 and 7 red because the declaration is absent entirely, a real regression signal but NOT evidence about the collapse. ⓘ **REACHABILITY AUDIT (s2226's duty): arm 6 reddened in NO variant until a SIXTH was built to reach it** — until you try to break an arm it is indistinguishable from a correct one. **One prediction corrected:** the docstring first claimed a single over-general cure reds arms 3, 5 and 6; measured, those are three DIFFERENT variants reddening three different controls and no one variant reds all three. 💡 **REUSABLE: when a tool selects its corpus POSITIONALLY out of a filtered list, the filter is not the only thing that can be wrong — ask whether the INDEX still means what its docstring says once the list can contain more than one entry per producer.** `handoffs[1]` meant "the previous fire" only while every fire emitted exactly one handoff commit, and nothing enforced that; the convention that broke it — re-running the battery and correcting your own line-1 — is one this very law *prescribes*. **A positional index is an unenforced uniqueness assumption wearing an integer.** GATE: none — landed.
- 🔎 **F-2262-1 THE DRAIN ARM OF §3.0's FIRST COMMAND ACCEPTS A NEAR-MISS AS AN IDENTITY, AND SAYS SO ONLY WHEN A SECOND LEAF HAPPENS TO MATCH (s2262, NON-BLOCKING, CURED SAME FIRE).** `drain-block-check.mjs` resolves a leaf by bidirectional SUBSTRING and takes `hits[0]`. F-1250-1 made an exact match sort first, which fixed the case where the subject HAS a leaf; the residue is the case where it does NOT — with no exact match to promote, a **neighbouring** leaf wins on length and answers on the subject's behalf. 🔑 **AND THE CAVEAT THAT WOULD HAVE EXPOSED IT IS GATED ON THE WRONG CONDITION: F-1250-1 wrote the correct sentence — *"A CLEAR that names a leaf the reader did not ask about is the exact shape that made the false clearance readable as an affirmative answer"* — and then printed it only `if (hits.length > 1)`. The deciding condition is not how many leaves matched, it is whether the winner is YOUR leaf, so a subject with exactly ONE inexact hit gets a well-formed verdict about a different task with no caveat at all.** ⚙️ **THE ASYMMETRY BETWEEN THE TWO ARMS IS THE DEFECT, FOR THE THIRD TIME IN THIS FILE** (F-1597-1 and the blocked-arm note in `findLeaves` are the same shape): the `--queue` arm requires exact equality and correctly answers UNKNOWN; the drain arm takes `hits[0]`. **Same resolver, same board, two different answers about the SAME task — measured live: `--queue e3-fairground.md` → `? UNKNOWN — no goal leaf matches` (correct: `tasks/e3-fairground.md` exists with no leaf) while its done-move → `✅ CLEAR — lane-d-e3-fairground-mask-table [world-e3-fairground-mask] status="merged"`, a verdict about a DIFFERENT task.** 📊 **MEASURED over all 1,284 done-moves against 613 leaves: 30 resolve to an INEXACT winner, and 23 are BENIGN** — the done-move carries session/hash decoration `normalize()` does not strip (`shipped-s1049-lane-b-deploy-honest-and-serialized-ab528c3f` CONTAINS `lane-b-deploy-honest-and-serialized`), so the needle contains the leaf's key and the leaf really is the subject. ⚠️ **INEXACTNESS ALONE IS THEREFORE NOT THE DEFECT AND MUST NOT BE THE TRIGGER — requiring exact equality here is the over-general cure and would break all 23.** 🎯 **THE DECIDABLE TRIGGER is the one the board can PROVE: the subject names a real MASTER on disk that has NO leaf of its own — then the honest verdict is Goal Registration Law debt and any verdict at all is about somebody else. 7 live instances (`e3-fairground` ×3, `lane-boss-healthbar` ×2, `lane-e9-arsenal`, `stream-capture-duty`), and 5 of the 7 print NO caveat today.** ⚖️ **SEVERITY STATED HONESTLY AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED: LATENT for §2F and LIVE only for the direct-query path.** All 7 are legacy bare-dated done-moves that `dry-board-probe` classes below its convention start, so **0 of 7 reach its subject set** and no dry-board verdict this streak has printed was wrong — verified, not assumed. What is wrong is that a fire running §3.0's prescribed `drain-block-check <done-move>` by hand is told CLEAR about a leaf it did not ask about. 🚦 **DECLARES, DOES NOT REFUSE (F-2218-1's restraint):** the near-miss answer is often the useful one and this is §3.0's most-run command, so a refusal would red lawful drains and be excused into uselessness inside a week (F-1460-1). Scoped to the provable case rather than to every inexact winner for F-2224's reason — an always-on line here is the noise that decays a declaration into a formality. Placed BEFORE the verdict branches so it covers CLOSED, blocked, CLEAR and `--queue` alike. ⓘ **DECLARED LIMIT: keyed on the master EXISTING, so a subject whose master was deleted or renamed still resolves to a neighbour silently — that case cannot be told from ordinary decoration without the file, and a guess is worse than a stated gap.** ✅ **BEHAVIOUR-NEUTRAL BEFORE LANDING (F-1274-2): 1,405 real cases — every one of the 1,284 done-moves, 120 `--queue` masters and `--all` — 1,398 byte-identical on stdout+stderr+rc, and the 7 that moved are EXAC