- ✅ **[F-1636-4 — s1636, CLOSES THIS THREAD AND RETIRES F-1631-3: THE WALL WAS STALE AND THE OWNER NEED SPEND NOTHING.** `gpt-5.6-sol` **ANSWERS RIGHT NOW** — probed s1636 at 19:11 local with the runner's own client: `OpenAI Codex v0.145.0 · model: gpt-5.6-sol · reply: OK · 13,008 tokens`. And it had already been proven two hours earlier by the artifacts: **all three 18:17 runs (ap16-1 lane-a 494,754 tokens, ap16-2 lane-d 438,317, ap16-3 lane-c 220,739) carry the banner `model: gpt-5.6-sol`** and none hit a usage limit — one of them is now MERGED at `8465f6b3`. 🔑 **THE ROOT CAUSE, WHICH IS A LAW DEFECT NOT A LAPSE: the fire-side probe and the lane runner do not share a codex binary.** Three installs exist — `/opt/homebrew/bin/codex` = **0.133.0** (what a fire's bare `codex` resolves to; `which -a` finds only this), `~/.nvm/.../v23.11.1` = **0.145.0** (what the runner uses; `lane-runner-v3.sh:173` invokes bare `codex`, so PATH decides), `~/.nvm/.../v24.14.0` = 0.115.0. The fire's 0.133.0 is **below the 0.144.1 floor `lane-runner-v3.sh:172` names for knowing 5.6 ids**, so a fire-side `-m gpt-5.6-sol` probe returns HTTP 400 *"requires a newer version"* **whatever the quota is**. ➡️ **§2.0's prescribed probe is therefore structurally incapable of ever reporting the wall lifted, which is why the wall outlived its cause by ~3 hours.** ⭐ **s1635 was one inch away and deserves the credit**: it proved the RUNNER was v0.145.0 and refuted the version theory correctly — it just assumed the probe shared that binary, so it filed the probe's HTTP 400 as noise when it was a true statement about a *different, genuinely too-old* client. Both halves were right about different binaries. **It is s1635's own lesson one level down: a probe sharing a defaulted parameter with its subject stops measuring the subject — there the MODEL, here the PATH.** 📋 **ACTIONS TAKEN s1636:** `tasks/CODEX-WALL` **DELETED** per §2.0, archived first to `logs/runs-archive/wall-evidence/CODEX-WALL-s1631-to-s16